[[:db/cas 17592186447153 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{16{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta +16{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{16{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta (1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{16{\\sec }^{4}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4[\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{16{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta +16{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{16{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta (1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{16{\\sec }^{4}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4\\left[\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)\\right] \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186447156 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +\\cancel{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -\\cancel{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +\\cancel{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -\\cancel{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186447157 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi }\\sqrt{{(2{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right))}^{2}+{(-2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)+{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right){\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\left(2{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)+{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right){\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186447206 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ rolls of the die\n\n- the probability of getting an even number (i.e., a $2,$ $4,$ $6,$ or $8$) on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{8}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of times we get an even number is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =1.732\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ rolls of the die\n\n- the probability of getting an even number (i.e., a $2,$ $4,$ $6,$ or $8$) on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{8}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B\\left(12,\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of times we get an even number is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =1.732\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186447295 :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=y-3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y(\\frac{1}{3}-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=3,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=\\frac{1}{3}.$" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=y-3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y\\left(\\frac{1}{3}-y\\right).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=3,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186447347 :answer/value "(0,\\frac{3}{2})" "\\left(0,\\frac{3}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186447349 :answer/value "(\\frac{5+\\sqrt{5}}{2},\\sqrt{5})" "\\left(\\frac{5+\\sqrt{5}}{2},\\sqrt{5}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186447352 :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{3},3)" "\\left(-\\frac{1}{3},3\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186447359 :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+1}{2},1)" "\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+1}{2},1\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186447364 :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4},2)" "\\left(-\\frac{1}{4},2\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186447374 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2} & =3-2y \\\\ x{y}^{2}+2y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}+2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2xy+2)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i,$ we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((2xy+2)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2xy+2 & =0 \\\\ xy+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2} & =3-2y \\\\ x{\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{2} & =3-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =3+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we get $y=3,$ and the corresponding point is $(-\\frac{1}{3},3).$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2} & =3-2y \\\\ x{y}^{2}+2y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}+2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2xy+2)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i,$ we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((2xy+2)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2xy+2 & =0 \\\\ xy+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2} & =3-2y \\\\ x{\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{2} & =3-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =3+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we get $y=3,$ and the corresponding point is $\\left(-\\frac{1}{3},3\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186447477 :answer/value "$(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{1}{5})$ and $(-\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{1}{6})$" "$\\left(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186447480 :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2})" "\\left(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186447496 :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{1}{5})$" "$(0,0,0)$ and $\\left(