{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186447587 :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{19}{5})" "\\left(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{19}{5}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186447596 :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})$" "$(0,0,0)$ and $\\left(\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186447628 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=4xy-2{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,4)-f(1,1,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,4⟩-⟨1,1,2⟩) \\\\ 0-(-4) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4y,4x,-4z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{2},4{c}_{1},-4{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =⟨4{c}_{2},4{c}_{1},-4{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =4{c}_{2}+12{c}_{1}-8{c}_{3} \\\\ 1 & =3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=3t+1,\\,z=2t+2,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t+1,\\,{c}_{2}=3t+1,\\,{c}_{3}=2t+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t+1)+(3t+1)-2(2t+2) & =1 \\\\ 3t+3+3t+1-4t-4 & =1 \\\\ 2t & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1=\\frac{5}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},3).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=4xy-2{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,4)-f(1,1,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,4⟩-⟨1,1,2⟩) \\\\ 0-(-4) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4y,4x,-4z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{2},4{c}_{1},-4{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =⟨4{c}_{2},4{c}_{1},-4{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,3,2⟩ \\\\ 4 & =4{c}_{2}+12{c}_{1}-8{c}_{3} \\\\ 1 & =3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=3t+1,\\,z=2t+2,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t+1,\\,{c}_{2}=3t+1,\\,{c}_{3}=2t+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t+1)+(3t+1)-2(2t+2) & =1 \\\\ 3t+3+3t+1-4t-4 & =1 \\\\ 2t & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1=\\frac{5}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},3\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186447629 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xy-{z}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,2,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,2,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨-1,2,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ -1-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{(-1)} & =\\frac{y-0}{2}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ -x & =\\frac{y}{2}=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=-t\\,\\,y=2t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=2t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2t+2(-t)+3(-t)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -4t+3{t}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-4t+1 & =0 \\\\ (3t-1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}).\n$$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(1)=2, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(-1,2,-1).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xy-{z}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,2,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,2,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨-1,2,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ -1-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨{c}_{2},{c}_{1},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{(-1)} & =\\frac{y-0}{2}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ -x & =\\frac{y}{2}=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=-t\\,\\,y=2t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=2t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2t+2(-t)+3(-t)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ -4t+3{t}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-4t+1 & =0 \\\\ (3t-1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(1)=2, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(-1,2,-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186447631 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+yz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨1,2,3⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3,4)-f(1,2,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3,4⟩-⟨1,2,3⟩) \\\\ 20-8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x,z,y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1},{c}_{3},{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 12 & =⟨4{c}_{1},{c}_{3},{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 12 & =4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{1}=\\frac{z-3}{1} \\\\ x-1 & =y-2=z-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=t+2,\\,z=t+3,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t+1,\\,{c}_{2}=t+2,\\,{c}_{3}=t+3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $12=4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(t+1)+(t+2)+(t+3) & =12 \\\\ 4t+4+t+2+t+3 & =12 \\\\ 6t+9 & =12 \\\\ 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2}).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+yz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨1,2,3⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3,4)-f(1,2,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3,4⟩-⟨1,2,3⟩) \\\\ 20-8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x,z,y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1},{c}_{3},{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 12 & =⟨4{c}_{1},{c}_{3},{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 12 & =4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{1}=\\frac{z-3}{1} \\\\ x-1 & =y-2=z-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=t+2,\\,z=t+3,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t+1,\\,{c}_{2}=t+2,\\,{c}_{3}=t+3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $12=4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(t+1)+(t+2)+(t+3) & =12 \\\\ 4t+4+t+2+t+3 & =12 \\\\ 6t+9 & =12 \\\\ 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186447634 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}-xz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,-1,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,-1,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-z,3{y}^{2},-x⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-{c}_{3},3{c}_{2}^{2},-{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨-{c}_{3},3{c}_{2}^{2},-{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =-{c}_{3}-3{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-1)}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ x & =-y=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t\\,\\,y=-t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=-t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{3}-3{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(-t)-3(-t)^{2}+t & =0 \\\\ t-3{t}^{2}+t & =0 \\\\ 2t-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(2-3t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}={c}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(0,0,0).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}).\n$$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}-xz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,-1,-1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,-1,-1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-z,3{y}^{2},-x⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-{c}_{3},3{c}_{2}^{2},-{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨-{c}_{3},3{c}_{2}^{2},-{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =-{c}_{3}-3{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-1)}=\\frac{z-0}{(-1)} \\\\ x & =-y=-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t\\,\\,y=-t,\\,z=-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=-t,\\,{c}_{3}=-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{3}-3{c}_{2}^{2}+{c}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(-t)-3(-t)^{2}+t & =0 \\\\ t-3{t}^{2}+t & =0 \\\\ 2t-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(2-3t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}={c}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(0,0,0).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186447637 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-xy+1$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ -1-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨3{x}^{2}-y,-x⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2},-{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =⟨3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2},-{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{1-0} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2} & =x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}=-2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-(2{c}_{1}+1)-2{c}_{1} & =-2 \\\\ 3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-4{c}_{1}+1 & =0 \\\\ (3{c}_{1}-1)({c}_{1}-1) & =0 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+1=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the first point are $(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3}).$\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=2(1)+1=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the second point are $(1,3).$\n\nFinally we can conclude that the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem are $(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3})$ and $(1,3).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-xy+1$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ -1-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨3{x}^{2}-y,-x⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2},-{c}_{1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =⟨3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2},-{c}_{1}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ -2 & =3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{1-0} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2} & =x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}=-2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-(2{c}_{1}+1)-2{c}_{1} & =-2 \\\\ 3{{c}_{1}}^{2}-4{c}_{1}+1 & =0 \\\\ (3{c}_{1}-1)({c}_{1}-1) & =0 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+1=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the first point are $\\left(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3}\\right).$\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=2(1)+1=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the second point are $(1,3).$\n\nFinally we can conclude that the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem are $\\left(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{5}{3}\\right)$ and $(1,3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186447640 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,4)-f(0,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,4⟩-⟨0,3⟩) \\\\ 3(4)^{2}+3(4)+2(3)^{2}-(3(3)^{2}+0(3)+2(0)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x+y,x+6y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =12{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2} \\\\ 51 & =13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-3}{4-3} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}=51,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13{c}_{1}+9(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3) & =51 \\\\ 13{c}_{1}+3{c}_{1}+27 & =51 \\\\ 16{c}_{1} & =24 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find $$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2}).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,4)-f(0,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,4⟩-⟨0,3⟩) \\\\ 3(4)^{2}+3(4)+2(3)^{2}-(3(3)^{2}+0(3)+2(0)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x+y,x+6y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =12{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2} \\\\ 51 & =13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-3}{4-3} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}=51,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13{c}_{1}+9\\left(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3\\right) & =51 \\\\ 13{c}_{1}+3{c}_{1}+27 & =51 \\\\ 16{c}_{1} & =24 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find $$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $\\left(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186447641 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,5⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,5)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,5⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 40-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{4}=\\frac{z-0}{5} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{z}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,\\,y=4t,\\,z=5t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=4t,\\,{c}_{3}=5t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}=40,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4t)(5t)+4(2t)(5t)+5(2t)(4t) & =40 \\\\ 40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ 120{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<1,$ it's clear that $t=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nFor $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$  This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =4\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =5\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}).\n$$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,4,5⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,4,5)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,4,5⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 40-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =⟨{c}_{2}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{3},{c}_{1}{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,5⟩ \\\\ 40 & =2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{4}=\\frac{z-0}{5} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{z}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,\\,y=4t,\\,z=5t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=4t,\\,{c}_{3}=5t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{2}{c}_{3}+4{c}_{1}{c}_{3}+5{c}_{1}{c}_{2}=40,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(4t)(5t)+4(2t)(5t)+5(2t)(4t) & =40 \\\\ 40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2}+40{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ 120{t}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<1,$ it's clear that $t=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nFor $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$  This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =4\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =5\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186449319 :answer-field/key "$f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" "$f\\left(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$"] [:db/cas 17592186450949 :answer-field/key "${(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4}=1$" "${\\left(\\frac{z}{w}\\right)}^{4}=1$"] [:db/cas 17592186451060 :answer-field/key "${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{5})<0$" "${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)<0$"] [:db/cas 17592186451106 :answer-field/key "${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2})=0$" "${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186451118 :answer-field/key "${f}^{″}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})>0$" "${f}^{″}\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)>0$"] [:db/cas