\pi }{2},\\pi )$" "$f(x)$ is concave down on $\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi \\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186454437 :answer-field/key "The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" "The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $\\left(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186454438 :answer-field/key "${f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ is defined" "${f}_{y}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0\\right)$ is defined"] [:db/cas 17592186454439 :answer-field/key "${f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is defined" "${f}_{z}\\left(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ is defined"] [:db/cas 17592186454997 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}})" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186455105 :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}})" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186455116 :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}})" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186455443 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡2\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡2\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-(0+1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455447 :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j\\right)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455449 :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=f(x),\\,y(2)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $3$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(2)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=f(x),\\,y(2)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $3$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(2)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left({a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x\\right)\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455449 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(2)=6.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & =A{e}^{-4(2)}+{y}_{p}(2) \\\\ 6 & =A{e}^{-8}+0 \\\\ A & =6{e}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(6{e}^{8}){e}^{-4x}=6{e}^{8-4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼6{e}^{8-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)).\n$$" "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left({a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(2)=6.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & =A{e}^{-4(2)}+{y}_{p}(2) \\\\ 6 & =A{e}^{-8}+0 \\\\ A & =6{e}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(6{e}^{8}){e}^{-4x}=6{e}^{8-4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼6{e}^{8-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left({a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x\\right)\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455453 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$, where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{(-1)}{2}-(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$, where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\left[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\right]}_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{(-1)}{2}-\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455471 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }$, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }$, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{\\cos }^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\left[\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\right]}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455480 :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,y(0)=-4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an even, $4$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$" "$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,y(0)=-4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an even, $4$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455480 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an even function and the differential operator contains no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits the symmetry and is also an even function consisting only of cosine terms. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+2=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{a}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x).\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{2}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we use the condition $y(0)=-4.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x).\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=-4$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =-4-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A$ back into ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(-4-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$" "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an even function and the differential operator contains no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits the symmetry and is also an even function consisting only of cosine terms. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+2=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{a}_{n}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x\\right).\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\pi }{2}{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{2}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we use the condition $y(0)=-4.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x\\right).\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=-4$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =-4-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A$ back into ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(-4-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186455509 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (3{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =27{\\sin }^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}27{\\sin }^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{\\sin }^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{\\sin }^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =81\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{6}-{0}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In pola