oint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{\\left[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{\\left[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}}_{f(x,y)}=\\underbrace{(3{x}^{2})}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}\\right)}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186478445 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(1)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(1)$ and ${r}^{″}(1)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(1)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 1,\\frac{1}{t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 0,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(1)$ and ${r}^{″}(1)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478451 :question/worked-solution "We're given $\\text{E}[Y].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $X$ when $0\\le x\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[X]+\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're given $\\text{E}[Y].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $X$ when $0\\le x\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x{\\left[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\left[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[X]+\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478456 :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\n \n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =1 \\\\ R & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$" "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\n \n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =1 \\\\ R & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478458 :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3b}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2y{e}^{-3x}}_{f(x,y)}=\\underbrace{(3{e}^{-3x})}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}{\\left[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}\\left(2-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\left[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}\\right]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =2y\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }\\left[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3b}+\\frac{1}{3}\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2y{e}^{-3x}}_{f(x,y)}=\\underbrace{(3{e}^{-3x})}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}y\\right)}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186478463 :question/worked-solution "We're given $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[Y].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+y}{21}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+xy]}_{x=-3}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}[(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}+3y)-(\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}-3y)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}[18+6y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2(3+y)}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{2(3+y)}{7}.\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3y+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}{[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}(\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{11}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3X+7Y] & =3\\,\\text{E}[X]+7\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3(0)+7\\left(\\frac{11}{21}\\right) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We're given $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[Y].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $Y$ when $0\\le y\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+y}{21}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}{\\left[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+xy\\right]}_{x=-3}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}\\left[\\left(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}+3y\\right)-\\left(\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}-3y\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}[18+6y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2(3+y)}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $Y$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y}(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{2(3+y)}{7}.\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3y+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}{\\left[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{11}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3X+7Y] & =3\\,\\text{E}[X]+7\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3(0)+7\\left(\\frac{11}{21}\\right) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186478464 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the expression as a square:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}={(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the square and the square root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}})}^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" "First, we rewrite the expression as a square:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}={\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}\\right)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the square and the square root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{5}}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479058 :question/problem "${(8\\times \\frac{3}{5})}^{4}=$" "${\\left(8\\times \\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{4}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186479058 :question/worked-solution "To raise the product of two numbers to the power of $4$, we can raise each number to the power of $4,$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n{(8\\times \\frac{3}{5})}^{4}={8}^{4}\\times {\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{4}\n$$" "To raise the product of two numbers to the power of $4$, we can raise each number to the power of $4,$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n{\\left(8\\times \\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{4}={8}^{4}\\times {\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{4}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479070 :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right){|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(1-0)+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}+(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right){|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(1-0)+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\left(1-\\frac{1}{a}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}+(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479078 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}÷2-\\frac{1}{3} &= (\\frac{4}{3}÷\\frac{2}{1})-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= (\\frac{4}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{2})-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{4\\times 1}{3\\times 2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{6}-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{6}-\\frac{1}{3} &= \\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{2-1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}÷2-\\frac{1}{3} &= \\left(\\frac{4}{3}÷\\frac{2}{1}\\right)-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{4\\times 1}{3\\times 2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{6}-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{6}-\\frac{1}{3} &= \\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{2-1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479079 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}\\times 3÷\\frac{1}{4} &= (\\frac{7}{2}\\times 3)÷\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ &= (\\frac{7}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{1})÷\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{7\\times 3}{2\\times 1}÷\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{21}{2}÷\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{21}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\ &= \\frac{21\\times 4}{2\\times 1} \\\\ &= \\frac{84}{2} \\\\ &= 42\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe multiply and divide, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}\\times 3÷\\frac{1}{4} &= \\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\times 3\\right)÷\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{1}\\right)÷\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{7\\times 3}{2\\times 1}÷\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{21}{2}÷\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{21}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\ &= \\frac{21\\times 4}{2\\times 1} \\\\ &= \\frac{84}{2} \\\\ &= 42\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479096 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8÷\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-3 &= (8÷\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right))-3 \\\\ &= (\\frac{8}{1}\\times \\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right))-3 \\\\ &= -\\frac{8\\times 5}{1\\times 4}-3 \\\\ &= -\\frac{40}{4}-3 \\\\ &= -10-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10-3 & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we divide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8÷\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-3 &= \\left(8÷\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\right)-3 \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{8}{1}\\times \\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)\\right)-3 \\\\ &= -\\frac{8\\times 5}{1\\times 4}-3 \\\\ &= -\\frac{40}{4}-3 \\\\ &= -10-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10-3 & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479098 :question/problem "${(5\\times \\frac{2}{3})}^{8}=$" "${\\left(5\\times \\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{8}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186479098 :question/worked-solution "To raise the product of two numbers to the power of $8$, we can raise each number to the power of $8,$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n{(5\\times \\frac{2}{3})}^{8}={5}^{8}\\times {\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{8}\n$$" "To raise the product of two numbers to the power of $8$, we can raise each number to the power of $8,$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n{\\left(5\\times \\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{8}={5}^{8}\\times {\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{8}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479102 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{8}\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $P(4,\\frac{5}{4})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{5}{4},4).$" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{8}\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $P\\left(4,\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(\\frac{5}{4},4\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186479102 :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(4,\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5}{8}\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{5}{16\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{5}{32}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(4,\\frac{5}{4})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{5}{4},4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{5}{32}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $\\left(4,\\frac{5}{4}\\right).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5}{8}\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{5}{16\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{5}{32}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left(4,\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $\\left(\\frac{5}{4},4\\right)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}\\left({f}^{-1}\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{5}{32}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479115 :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-[4+\\frac{32}{5}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(-\\frac{52}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}\\right){|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(0-\\left[4+\\frac{32}{5}\\right]\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\left(-\\frac{52}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479121 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe add and subtract, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}+5-\\frac{7}{6} &= (\\frac{4}{3}+5)-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ &= (\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{15}{3})-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ &= \\frac{4+15}{3}-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ &= \\frac{19}{3}-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ &= \\frac{38}{6}-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ &= \\frac{38-7}{6} \\\\ &= \\frac{31}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe add and subtract, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}+5-\\frac{7}{6} &= \\left(\\frac{4}{3}+5\\right)-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{15}{3}\\right)-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ &= \\frac{4+15}{3}-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ &= \\frac{19}{3}-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ &= \\frac{38}{6}-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ &= \\frac{38-7}{6} \\\\ &= \\frac{31}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479127 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe divide and multiply, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}÷\\frac{2}{5}\\times 7 &= (\\frac{3}{7}÷\\frac{2}{5})\\times 7 \\\\ &= (\\frac{3}{7}\\times \\frac{5}{2})\\times 7 \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\times 5}{7\\times 2}\\times 7 \\\\ &= \\frac{15}{14}\\times 7 \\\\ &= \\frac{15}{14}\\times \\frac{7}{1} \\\\ &= \\frac{15\\times 7}{14\\times 1} \\\\ &= \\frac{105}{14} \\\\ &= \\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe divide and multiply, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}÷\\frac{2}{5}\\times 7 &= \\left(\\frac{3}{7}÷\\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\times 7 \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\times \\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\times 7 \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\times 5}{7\\times 2}\\times 7 \\\\ &= \\frac{15}{14}\\times 7 \\\\ &= \\frac{15}{14}\\times \\frac{7}{1} \\\\ &= \\frac{15\\times 7}{14\\times 1} \\\\ &= \\frac{105}{14} \\\\ &= \\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479134 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe add and subtract, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}+3-\\frac{5}{8} &= (\\frac{1}{4}+3)-\\frac{5}{8} \\\\ &= (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{12}{4})-\\frac{5}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{1+12}{4}-\\frac{5}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{13}{4}-\\frac{5}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{26}{8}-\\frac{5}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{26-5}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{21}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe add and subtract, from left to right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}+3-\\frac{5}{8} &= \\left(\\frac{1}{4}+3\\right)-\\frac{5}{8} \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{12}{4}\\right)-\\frac{5}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{1+12}{4}-\\frac{5}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{13}{4}-\\frac{5}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{26}{8}-\\frac{5}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{26-5}{8} \\\\ &= \\frac{21}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479842 :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}y}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,j)\\cdot (\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{t+3}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}y}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,j\\right)\\cdot (\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{t+3}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479852 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=\\sqrt{2}$ given that $$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=(\\operatorname{ln}(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x}{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$" "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=\\sqrt{2}$ given that $$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\left(\\operatorname{ln}(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x}{x+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186479852 :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y})i+\\frac{x}{x+y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=1-2t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1-2t)+\\frac{2t}{2t+1-2t})i+\\frac{2t}{2t+1-2t}\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+(-2)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2t\\,i+2t\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+(-2)\\,j) \\\\  & =2t\\cdot 2+2t\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4t-4t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\sqrt{2}))] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y}\\right)i+\\frac{x}{x+y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=1-2t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\left(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1-2t)+\\frac{2t}{2t+1-2t}\\right)i+\\frac{2t}{2t+1-2t}\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+(-2)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2t\\,i+2t\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+(-2)\\,j) \\\\  & =2t\\cdot 2+2t\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4t-4t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\sqrt{2}))] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479859 :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{3}$ and $y=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2t}+\\frac{t}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2}t \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{3}$ and $y=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j\\right)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2t}+\\frac{t}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2}t \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479863 :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(x+y)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(x+y)^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=3t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(2t+3t)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(2t+3t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(5t)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(5t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2+\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+25{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{5}{1+25(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+25} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y))\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{1+(x+y)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(x+y)^{2}}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=3t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{1+(2t+3t)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(2t+3t)^{2}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{1+(5t)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(5t)^{2}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle \\cdot ⟨2,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2+\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+25{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{5}{1+25(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+25} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479870 :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ if ${f}_{yy}(x,y)=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ if ${f}_{yy}(x,y)=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$"] [:db/cas 17592186479870 :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{yyx}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+(-{x}^{2})\\cdot (-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxy}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+(1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\,\n$$" "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{yyx}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+(-{x}^{2})\\cdot (-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxy}\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+(1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\,\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479876 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\frac{1}{t},t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\left\\langle {t}^{2},\\frac{1}{t},t\\right\\rangle $ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186479876 :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z),-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y=\\frac{1}{t},$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},1⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot 2t+(-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z))\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z),-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y=\\frac{1}{t},$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 2t,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},1\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\right\\rangle \\cdot \\left\\langle 2t,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},1\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\right)\\cdot 2t+(-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left[f\\left(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\right)\\right] & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186479889 :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{y}+z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y},x{e}^{y},1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=4,$ $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t},4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t},1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,4t,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{1}{t},{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨t,4t,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\frac{1}{t},{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =t\\cdot 0+4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+1\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =4+{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =4+{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{y}+z)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y},x{e}^{y},1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=4,$ $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t},4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t},1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,4t,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),{e}^{t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 0,\\frac{1}{t},{e}^{t}\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨t,4t,1⟩\\cdot \\left\\langle 0,\\frac{1}{t},{e}^{t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =t\\cdot 0+4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+1\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =4+{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =4+{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480008 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡3} & ={(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡3} & ={\\left(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}\\right)\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480011 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}} & ={(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}} & ={\\left(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}\\right)\\,|}_{x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480016 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln ⁡7$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}+\\ln ⁡7) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡7) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡9} & ={(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln ⁡7$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}+\\ln ⁡7\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡7) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡9} & ={\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}\\right)\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480017 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡2} & ={(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}+2\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡2} & ={\\left(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}\\right)\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480023 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{12}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{12}{5}} & ={(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{12}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{12}{5}} & ={\\left(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}\\right)\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480027 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln ⁡5$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡5+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡5)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}} & ={(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln ⁡5$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\ln ⁡5+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡5)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}\\right) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}\\right)\\,|}_{x=\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{\\ln ⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480034 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{2}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{2}{3}} & ={(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}+2\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{2}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{2}{3}} & ={\\left(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}\\right)\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480041 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln ⁡7$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}+\\ln ⁡7) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡7) \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{11}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{11}{2}} & ={(- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{11} \\\\  & =- \\frac{16}{55}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln ⁡7$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}+\\ln ⁡7\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡7) \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{11}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{11}{2}} & ={\\left(- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}\\right)\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{11} \\\\  & =- \\frac{16}{55}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480044 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln ⁡5$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡5+\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡5)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{7}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡2} & ={(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln ⁡5$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\ln ⁡5+\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡5)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}\\right) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{7}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡2} & ={\\left(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}\\right)\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480048 :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡5$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡5} & ={(\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x}-11\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-\\ln ⁡5$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡5} & ={\\left(\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x}\\right)\\,|}_{x=-\\ln ⁡5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{-\\ln ⁡5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480159 :answer/value "⟨\\frac{12}{7},\\frac{8}{7},-\\frac{4}{7}⟩" "\\left\\langle \\frac{12}{7},\\frac{8}{7},-\\frac{4}{7}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186480335 :answer/value "⟨\\frac{24}{101},\\frac{16}{101},-\\frac{32}{101}⟩" "\\left\\langle \\frac{24}{101},\\frac{16}{101},-\\frac{32}{101}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186480400 :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{7},\\frac{2}{7},-\\frac{1}{7}⟩" "\\left\\langle \\frac{3}{7},\\frac{2}{7},-\\frac{1}{7}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186480411 :answer/value "⟨\\frac{6}{7},\\frac{4}{7},-\\frac{2}{7}⟩" "\\left\\langle \\frac{6}{7},\\frac{4}{7},-\\frac{2}{7}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186480675 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{+}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{f}^{+}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{0+(-3)}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0 \\\\ {f}^{+}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{f}^{+}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{0+(-3)}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480676 :question/problem "The number $y(t)$ of homes in a city that have adopted clean energy follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y(20-\\frac{y}{125}),\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy?\n\nThe maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy is\n{{field-1}}." "The number $y(t)$ of homes in a city that have adopted clean energy follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y\\left(20-\\frac{y}{125}\\right),\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy?\n\nThe maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186480676 :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of clean-energy-adopting homes in the city. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y(20-\\frac{y}{125}) \\\\  & =3y\\left(\\frac{2\\,500-y}{125}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{125}(2\\,500-y) \\\\  & =0.024y(2\\,500-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2500.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2500,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy is $2500.$" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of clean-energy-adopting homes in the city. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y\\left(20-\\frac{y}{125}\\right) \\\\  & =3y\\left(\\frac{2\\,500-y}{125}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{125}(2\\,500-y) \\\\  & =0.024y(2\\,500-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2500.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2500,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy is $2500.$"] [:db/cas 17592186480684 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-3) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}f(x)=4 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-3) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}f(x)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-3)+{f}^{+}(-3)}{2}=\\frac{4+(-2)}{2}=1.\n$$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-3) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}f(x)=4 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-3) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}f(x)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-3)+{f}^{+}(-3)}{2}=\\frac{4+(-2)}{2}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480692 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{p}\\,q & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{p\\cdot p}p \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}p \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 6+2\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 8}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}p \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{14}⟨3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}⟨3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{12}{7},\\frac{8}{7},-\\frac{4}{7}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{p}\\,q & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{p\\cdot p}p \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}p \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 6+2\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 8}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}p \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{14}⟨3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}⟨3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{12}{7},\\frac{8}{7},-\\frac{4}{7}\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186480697 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y=y(t):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9y(1-\\frac{y}{1\\,200}),\\,y(0)=200\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)?$" "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y=y(t):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9y\\left(1-\\frac{y}{1\\,200}\\right),\\,y(0)=200\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186480697 :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9y(1-\\frac{y}{1\\,200}) \\\\  & =9y\\left(\\frac{1\\,200-y}{1\\,200}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9y}{1\\,200}(1\\,200-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{400}(1\\,200-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,200.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,200.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)=1\\,200.\n$$" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9y\\left(1-\\frac{y}{1\\,200}\\right) \\\\  & =9y\\left(\\frac{1\\,200-y}{1\\,200}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9y}{1\\,200}(1\\,200-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{400}(1\\,200-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,200.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,200.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)=1\\,200.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481343 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-\\frac{1}{3}),$  $(2,-\\frac{2}{3}),$  $(2,-\\frac{1}{3}),$ and $(1,0).$" "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $\\left(1,-\\frac{1}{3}\\right),$  $\\left(2,-\\frac{2}{3}\\right),$  $\\left(2,-\\frac{1}{3}\\right),$ and $(1,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186481343 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{x}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x+3y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{3}) & =(1,1+3\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =(1,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-\\frac{2}{3}) & =(2,2+3\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-\\frac{1}{3}) & =(2,2+3\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =(2,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1,1+3(0)) \\\\  & =(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/33/33a7fa05505942d0729e5a5be2bd691a49c242937fdb9de18f6a6f97b5799424.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{v}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x+3y}{x}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x+3y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\left(1,-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & =\\left(1,1+3\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =(1,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}\\left(2,-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) & =\\left(2,2+3\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}\\left(2,-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) & =\\left(2,2+3\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =(2,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1,1+3(0)) \\\\  & =(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/33/33a7fa05505942d0729e5a5be2bd691a49c242937fdb9de18f6a6f97b5799424.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x+3y}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{v}{u}\\,|\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{v}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481353 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle (7-5)^{-4}+{(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}=\n$${{field-1}}" "$$\n\\displaystyle (7-5)^{-4}+{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}=\n$${{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186481353 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. \n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7-5)^{-4}+{(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{0} &= {2}^{-4}+{(\\frac{6}{10}-\\frac{5}{10})}^{0} \\\\ &= {2}^{-4}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that ${\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{0} &= {2}^{-4}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that $$\n\\displaystyle {2}^{-4}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{16}.\n$$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+1 &= \\frac{1}{16}+1 \\\\ &= \\frac{1+16}{16} \\\\ &= \\frac{17}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. \n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7-5)^{-4}+{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0} &= {2}^{-4}+{\\left(\\frac{6}{10}-\\frac{5}{10}\\right)}^{0} \\\\ &= {2}^{-4}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that ${\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{0} &= {2}^{-4}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that $$\n\\displaystyle {2}^{-4}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{16}.\n$$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-4}+1 &= \\frac{1}{16}+1 \\\\ &= \\frac{1+16}{16} \\\\ &= \\frac{17}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481355 :question/problem "${7}^{-1}\\times (2+\\frac{1}{3})=$" "${7}^{-1}\\times \\left(2+\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186481355 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${7}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{-1}\\times (2+\\frac{1}{3}) &= \\frac{1}{7}\\times (2+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{6+1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{1\\times 7}{7\\times 3} \\\\ &= \\frac{1\\times \\cancel{7}}{3\\times \\cancel{7}} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${7}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{-1}\\times \\left(2+\\frac{1}{3}\\right) &= \\frac{1}{7}\\times \\left(2+\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{6+1}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{1\\times 7}{7\\times 3} \\\\ &= \\frac{1\\times \\cancel{7}}{3\\times \\cancel{7}} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481357 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=4\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta (4-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2[(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\cdot 4\\pi =1.\n$$" "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=4\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{\\left[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}\\right]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta (4-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\left[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\right]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\left[(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)\\right] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\cdot 4\\pi =1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481364 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}=\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1+0,1-0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,1) & =(2+1,2-1) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3+0,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2+(-1),2-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f3/f34926c196b850fa22e0f918b27c8d25e5deb7f6aa7ec232e2eeaf4e46dd309c.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}=\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(1+0,1-0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,1) & =(2+1,2-1) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3+0,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2+(-1),2-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f3/f34926c196b850fa22e0f918b27c8d25e5deb7f6aa7ec232e2eeaf4e46dd309c.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+y}{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{u}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}\\right]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481367 :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{x}^{2}y]_{x=0}^{x=y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{16}]_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=3.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =f(a,3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =(2a\\cdot 3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =24a \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{2}{3},3)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{2}{3}.$" "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{x}^{2}y]_{x=0}^{x=y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{16}\\right]_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=3.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =f(a,3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =(2a\\cdot 3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =24a \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\left(\\frac{2}{3},3\\right)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{2}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186481368 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle 6{x}^{3}(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle 6{x}^{3}\\left(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481368 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{3}(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}) &= (6{x}^{3})\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+(6{x}^{3})\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= (6{x}^{3})(\\frac{1}{2}x)+(6{x}^{3})(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\ &= (6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})({x}^{3}\\cdot x)+(6\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right))({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\ &= (3)({x}^{3+1})+(-2)\\cdot ({x}^{3+3}) \\\\ &= 3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{3}\\left(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right) &= (6{x}^{3})\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+(6{x}^{3})\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\ &= (6{x}^{3})\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)+(6{x}^{3})\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\left(6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)({x}^{3}\\cdot x)+\\left(6\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\ &= (3)({x}^{3+1})+(-2)\\cdot ({x}^{3+3}) \\\\ &= 3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481371 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x},\\,v={e}^{y}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }{e}^{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x+y}}=\\frac{1}{uv}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{uv}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}{[\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡v}{2}]}_{1}^{4}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{\\ln }^{2}⁡4-{\\ln }^{2}⁡1]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡4}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,du \\\\  & =2{\\ln }^{2}⁡2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{\\ln }^{2}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =2{\\ln }^{3}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x},\\,v={e}^{y}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }{e}^{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x+y}}=\\frac{1}{uv}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{uv}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\left[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{v}\\,dv\\right]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}{\\left[\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡v}{2}\\right]}_{1}^{4}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}[{\\ln }^{2}⁡4-{\\ln }^{2}⁡1]\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡4}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,du \\\\  & =2{\\ln }^{2}⁡2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{\\ln }^{2}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =2{\\ln }^{3}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481373 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}-11\\times {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-3}.\n$$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}^{0}-11\\times {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481373 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, notice that\n$\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16}$\nis not zero. Any non-zero number raised to the power of $0$ equals $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n{(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-3}={\\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right)}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16})}^{0}-11\\times {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-3} &= 1-11\\times 8 \\\\ &= 1-88 \\\\ &= -87\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, notice that\n$\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16}$\nis not zero. Any non-zero number raised to the power of $0$ equals $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the negative exponent and get\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-3}={\\left(\\frac{2}{1}\\right)}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting all these values into the expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{1}{16}\\right)}^{0}-11\\times {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-3} &= 1-11\\times 8 \\\\ &= 1-88 \\\\ &= -87\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481378 :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(3y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[3xy-{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =f(1,b)\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 18 & =(3b-2\\cdot 1)\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 4 & =3b-2 \\\\ 6 & =3b \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(1,2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=2.$" "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(3y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[3xy-{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =f(1,b)\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 18 & =(3b-2\\cdot 1)\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 4 & =3b-2 \\\\ 6 & =3b \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(1,2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186481380 :question/problem "${(18\\times \\frac{1}{3})}^{2}÷(2+{0.03}^{0}\\,)-3=$" "${\\left(18\\times \\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}÷(2+{0.03}^{0}\\,)-3=$"] [:db/cas 17592186481380 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${0.03}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(18\\times \\frac{1}{3})}^{2}÷(2+{0.03}^{0}\\,)-3 &= {(18\\times \\frac{1}{3})}^{2}÷(2+1\\,)-3 \\\\ &= {6}^{2}÷(2+1\\,)-3 \\\\ &= {6}^{2}÷3-3 \\\\ &= 36÷3-3 \\\\ &= 12-3 \\\\ &= 9\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${0.03}^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(18\\times \\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}÷(2+{0.03}^{0}\\,)-3 &= {\\left(18\\times \\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}÷(2+1\\,)-3 \\\\ &= {6}^{2}÷(2+1\\,)-3 \\\\ &= {6}^{2}÷3-3 \\\\ &= 36÷3-3 \\\\ &= 12-3 \\\\ &= 9\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481384 :question/problem "${({\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2})}^{-2}=$" "${\\left({\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}\\right)}^{-2}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186481384 :question/worked-solution "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2})}^{-2}={\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2\\cdot (-2)}={\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{-4},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $\\frac{1}{6}$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{-4}={6}^{4}\n$$" "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\left({\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2}\\right)}^{-2}={\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2\\cdot (-2)}={\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{-4},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $\\frac{1}{6}$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{-4}={6}^{4}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481387 :question/problem "${({\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{-4})}^{6}=$" "${\\left({\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{-4}\\right)}^{6}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186481387 :question/worked-solution "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{-4})}^{6}={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{(-4)\\cdot 6}={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{-24}\n$$" "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\left({\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{-4}\\right)}^{6}={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{(-4)\\cdot 6}={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{-24}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481389 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡u}{2}]}_{1}^{4}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\ln }^{2}⁡4-{\\ln }^{2}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\ln }^{2}⁡4{\\int }_{0}^{2}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\ln }^{2}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2{\\ln }^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,du\\right]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\left[\\frac{{\\ln }^{2}⁡u}{2}\\right]}_{1}^{4}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\ln }^{2}⁡4-{\\ln }^{2}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\ln }^{2}⁡4{\\int }_{0}^{2}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\ln }^{2}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2{\\ln }^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481399 :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6x+9)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[3{x}^{2}+9x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot {2}^{2}+9\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 30 & =(2a+{1}^{2})\\cdot 6 \\\\ 30 & =12a+6 \\\\ 24 & =12a \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2,1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=2.$" "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\right]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6x+9)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[3{x}^{2}+9x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot {2}^{2}+9\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 30 & =(2a+{1}^{2})\\cdot 6 \\\\ 30 & =12a+6 \\\\ 24 & =12a \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2,1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186481401 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}a(9{a}^{4}-15a) &= (\\frac{1}{3}a)(9{a}^{4})+(\\frac{1}{3}a)(-15a) \\\\ &= (\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9)({a}^{1}\\cdot {a}^{4})+(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-15))(a\\cdot a) \\\\ &= (3)({a}^{1+4})+(-5)({a}^{1+1}) \\\\ &= 3{a}^{5}-5{a}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}a(9{a}^{4}-15a) &= \\left(\\frac{1}{3}a\\right)(9{a}^{4})+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}a\\right)(-15a) \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9\\right)({a}^{1}\\cdot {a}^{4})+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-15)\\right)(a\\cdot a) \\\\ &= (3)({a}^{1+4})+(-5)({a}^{1+1}) \\\\ &= 3{a}^{5}-5{a}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481402 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(3{x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[3{x}^{2}y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[2{x}^{3}-4x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\cdot {2}^{3}-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(3{x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\left[3{x}^{2}y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{y=1}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[2{x}^{3}-4x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\cdot {2}^{3}-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481403 :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4x+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{2}+8x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {2}^{2}+8\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 24 & =(2a+3\\cdot {1}^{2})\\cdot 4 \\\\ 6 & =2a+3 \\\\ 3 & =2a \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{3}{2},1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{3}{2}.$" "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4x+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{2}+8x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {2}^{2}+8\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 24 & =(2a+3\\cdot {1}^{2})\\cdot 4 \\\\ 6 & =2a+3 \\\\ 3 & =2a \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\left(\\frac{3}{2},1\\right)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{3}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186481404 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot x{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{3}{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{2y}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}60{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[30{e}^{2y}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =120-30 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis is $90\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot x{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[{\\int }_{2}^{4}{x}^{3}{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{2y}{\\left[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\right]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}60{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[30{e}^{2y}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =120-30 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis is $90\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186481405 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e570374b37c116e32c3a7762b8cf09a28bdbb3e06b5e67e25837902a89bcd264.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y+\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e570374b37c116e32c3a7762b8cf09a28bdbb3e06b5e67e25837902a89bcd264.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y+\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}\\right)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481407 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(1,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that is mind, let's now write the $y=-\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}5y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{5}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{5}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{[-\\frac{1}{6}{\\cos }^{6}⁡\\theta ]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}[{\\cos }^{6}⁡(0)-{\\cos }^{6}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{16}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{32}{3\\pi }.\n$$" "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(1,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that is mind, let's now write the $y=-\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}5y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{5}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{5}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\left[-\\frac{1}{6}{\\cos }^{6}⁡\\theta \\right]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}\\left[{\\cos }^{6}⁡(0)-{\\cos }^{6}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right] \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{16}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{32}{3\\pi }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481408 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{{e}^{1/4}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)\\pi =4(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1).\n$$" "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\left[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}\\right]}_{0}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\left(\\frac{{e}^{1/4}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)\\pi =4(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481410 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}-xy=x(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(3,2) & =(3,3-2) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,4) & =(5,5-4) \\\\  & =(5,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,2) & =(5,5-2) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e0/e0b810c03e7e3210fcff3a739e3781ad5ff694bfe44d5f73d7782c3b4108a286.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}x(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}-xy=x(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(3,2) & =(3,3-2) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,4) & =(5,5-4) \\\\  & =(5,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,2) & =(5,5-2) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e0/e0b810c03e7e3210fcff3a739e3781ad5ff694bfe44d5f73d7782c3b4108a286.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}x(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}\\right]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481411 :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{D}y+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2x}y+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{x}^{2}y]_{y=x}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(8-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{D}y+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2x}y+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{x}^{2}y\\right]_{y=x}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left[{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(8-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481418 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{\\cos }^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{\\cos }^{2}⁡u\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{\\cos }^{2}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2u+1)du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2u}{2}+u]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\pi v\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{\\cos }^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{\\cos }^{2}⁡u\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{\\cos }^{2}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2u+1)du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v\\left[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2u}{2}+u\\right]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\pi v\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\left[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481421 :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\int }_{y/2}^{y}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[{x}^{2}+xy]_{x=y/2}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}([{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}]-[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5{y}^{3}}{12}]}_{y=0}^{y=6} \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\int }_{y/2}^{y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[x]_{x=y/2}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=6} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=4.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =f(a,4)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 90 & =(2a+4)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 10 & =2a+4 \\\\ 6 & =2a \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(3,4)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=3.$" "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\int }_{y/2}^{y}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[{x}^{2}+xy]_{x=y/2}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\left([{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}]-\\left[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\right]\\right)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{5{y}^{3}}{12}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=6} \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\int }_{y/2}^{y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[x]_{x=y/2}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=6} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=4.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =f(a,4)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 90 & =(2a+4)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 10 & =2a+4 \\\\ 6 & =2a \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(3,4)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186481423 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ $(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}),$ $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}),$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}).$" "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $\\left(1,-\\frac{1}{2}\\right),$ $\\left(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}\\right),$ $\\left(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}\\right),$ and $\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186481423 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x,\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2\\left(\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2\\left(\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/dd/dd628fd99b4684d79e8c3b0f4e6696f72582abaef18eef729f5611711a2e42ec.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x,\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\left(1,-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =\\left(2\\cdot 1,1+2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) & =\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}\\right) & =\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2\\left(\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ {T}^{-1}\\left(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}\\right) & =\\left(2\\cdot 1,1+2\\left(\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/dd/dd628fd99b4684d79e8c3b0f4e6696f72582abaef18eef729f5611711a2e42ec.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}\\right]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186481430 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2}=(2x+y)(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-1) & =(2(1)+(-1),1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,0) & =(2(2)+0,2-0) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-2) & =(2(3)+(-2),3-(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,5) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-3) & =(2(2)+(-3),2-(-3)) \\\\  & =(1,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,2\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9f/9f5030bc3437fbc4431dd5350620bc7f9133d2bf910654607c0c5f7ef292f3a3.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{2}^{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{21}{2}\\cdot \\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2}=(2x+y)(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-1) & =(2(1)+(-1),1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,0) & =(2(2)+0,2-0) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-2) & =(2(3)+(-2),3-(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,5) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-3) & =(2(2)+(-3),2-(-3)) \\\\  & =(1,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,