"-{\\left(2x+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186483964 :answer/value "-{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}" "-{\\left(x+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186484005 :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}" "-2{\\left(x+\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186484021 :answer/value "z=\\frac{3}{2}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}" "z=\\frac{3}{2}{\\left(y-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}"] [:db/cas 17592186484037 :answer/value "-{(4x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}" "-{\\left(4x+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186484047 :answer/value "$(5,0,-6)$ and $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{14}{3})$" "$(5,0,-6)$ and $\\left(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{14}{3}\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186484200 :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}x\\cdot (2x-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(2{x}^{2}-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2})|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot (2x-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{6}-{\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}x\\cdot (2x-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(2{x}^{2}-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2}\\right)|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot (2x-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{6}-{\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484203 :question/problem "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,-$\n{{field-3}}${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}$" "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,-$\n{{field-3}}${\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186484203 :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we plug $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ and ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ and ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =\\sqrt{3}+1(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}+1(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we plug $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right){\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ and ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ and ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =\\sqrt{3}+1\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+(-\\sqrt{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}+1\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484208 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{5}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-4\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)}{\\frac{3}{5}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-4\\,k)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left(3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)\\,k\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)}{\\frac{3}{5}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484223 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-5}{2}=\\frac{y}{-1}=\\frac{z+6}{1}=t\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t+5 \\\\ y &=-t \\\\ z &=t-6,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ ((2t+5)+1)^{2}+(-t)^{2}+((t-6)-2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (2t+6)^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(t-8)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (4{t}^{2}+24t+36)+{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}-16t+64) & =100 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+8t+100 & =100 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+8t & =0 \\\\ 2t(3t+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-\\frac{4}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2(0)+5=5 \\\\ y &=-(0)=0 \\\\ z &=(0)-6=-6,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(5,0,-6).$\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{4}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+5=\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ y &=-\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)=\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ z &=\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)-6=-\\frac{22}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(5,0,-6)$ and $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3}).\n$$" "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-5}{2}=\\frac{y}{-1}=\\frac{z+6}{1}=t\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t+5 \\\\ y &=-t \\\\ z &=t-6,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ ((2t+5)+1)^{2}+(-t)^{2}+((t-6)-2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (2t+6)^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(t-8)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (4{t}^{2}+24t+36)+{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}-16t+64) & =100 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+8t+100 & =100 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+8t & =0 \\\\ 2t(3t+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-\\frac{4}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2(0)+5=5 \\\\ y &=-(0)=0 \\\\ z &=(0)-6=-6,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(5,0,-6).$\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{4}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+5=\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ y &=-\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)=\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ z &=\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)-6=-\\frac{22}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(5,0,-6)$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484225 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=116,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.6+5\\cdot 0.1+10\\cdot 0.2+15\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0+0.5+2+1.5 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.6+{5}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{10}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{15}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+2.5+20+22.5)-16 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(4,\\frac{29}{116}) \\\\  & ∼N(4,0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5) & ≈P(\\frac{3-4}{\\sqrt{0.25}}<Z<\\frac{5-4}{\\sqrt{0.25}}) \\\\  & =P(-2<Z<2) \\\\  & =Φ(2)-Φ(-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5)=Φ(2)-Φ(-2).\n$$" "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=116,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.6+5\\cdot 0.1+10\\cdot 0.2+15\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0+0.5+2+1.5 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.6+{5}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{10}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{15}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+2.5+20+22.5)-16 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\,\\left(4,\\frac{29}{116}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(4,0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5) & ≈P\\left(\\frac{3-4}{\\sqrt{0.25}}<Z<\\frac{5-4}{\\sqrt{0.25}}\\right) \\\\  & =P(-2<Z<2) \\\\  & =Φ(2)-Φ(-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5)=Φ(2)-Φ(-2).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484226 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2z-x-10=0\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{x}{2}+5\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =2{(\\frac{x}{2}+5-2)}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =2{(\\frac{x}{2}+3)}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+3x+9) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+6x+18 \\\\ \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}-6x+(y+3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4)-6+(y+3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{(x-2)}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{{(x-2)}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{24} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of an ellipse centered at $(2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane." "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2z-x-10=0\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{x}{2}+5\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+5-2\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =2{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+3\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+3x+9\\right) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+6x+18 \\\\ \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}-6x+(y+3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4)-6+(y+3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{(x-2)}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{{(x-2)}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{24} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of an ellipse centered at $(2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane."] [:db/cas 17592186484233 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y=2\\,⟹\\,x=2-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-y)^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+4+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{y}^{2}-4y+4 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4({y}^{2}-y+1) \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3 \\\\ 6z & =4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+9 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the parabola $$\n\\displaystyle z=\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y=2\\,⟹\\,x=2-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-y)^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+4+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{y}^{2}-4y+4 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4({y}^{2}-y+1) \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{\\left(y-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+3 \\\\ 6z & =4{\\left(y-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+9 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(y-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the parabola $$\n\\displaystyle z=\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(y-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484244 :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 4(-3t)^{2}+2(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2} & =12 \\\\ 36{t}^{2}+8{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 48{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-1 \\\\ z &=-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ z &=-2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1).$" "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 4(-3t)^{2}+2(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2} & =12 \\\\ 36{t}^{2}+8{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 48{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-1 \\\\ z &=-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1\\right).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ z &=-2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $\\left(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186484247 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+36({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(36-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\left(\\frac{324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{n}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}{9}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$" "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+36\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\left(36-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\left(\\frac{324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{n}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}{9}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484251 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{-2t}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}{{e}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{-t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨1,\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}{{e}^{t}}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}{{e}^{3t}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{-2t}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}{{e}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{-t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨1,\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}{{e}^{t}}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}{{e}^{3t}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484253 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{45}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{128}{45})-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{45}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =2.2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.66.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.2,\\frac{0.66}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.2,0.022).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =P(\\frac{2-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}<Z<\\frac{2.3-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.35<Z<0.67) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.67)-P(Z<-1.35) \\\\  & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.67)=0.7486, \\\\ Φ(-1.35)=0.0885.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35) \\\\  & =0.7486-0.0885 \\\\  & =0.6601.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}x\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}{x}^{2}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{45}\\right]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{128}{45}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{45}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =2.2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.66.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\,\\left(2.2,\\frac{0.66}{30}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(2.2,0.022).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =P\\left(\\frac{2-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}<Z<\\frac{2.3-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.35<Z<0.67) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.67)-P(Z<-1.35) \\\\  & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.67)=0.7486, \\\\ Φ(-1.35)=0.0885.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35) \\\\  & =0.7486-0.0885 \\\\  & =0.6601.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484259 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=121,\\,\\mu =300,\\,\\sigma =55.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(300,\\frac{{55}^{2}}{121}) \\\\  & ∼N(300,25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<295).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<295) & =P(Z<\\frac{295-300}{\\sqrt{25}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-\\frac{5}{5}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1) \\\\  & =Φ(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1)=0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<295) & =Φ(-1) \\\\  & =0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=121,\\,\\mu =300,\\,\\sigma =55.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\,\\left(300,\\frac{{55}^{2}}{121}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(300,25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<295).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<295) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{295-300}{\\sqrt{25}}\\right) \\\\  & =P\\left(Z<-\\frac{5}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1) \\\\  & =Φ(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1)=0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<295) & =Φ(-1) \\\\  & =0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484262 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=65,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 0.1+(-6)\\cdot 0.4+(-4)\\cdot 0.4+(-2)\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =-0.8-2.4-1.6-0.2 \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =((-8)^{2}\\cdot 0.1+(-6)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-(-5)^{2} \\\\  & =(6.4+14.4+6.4+0.4)-25 \\\\  & =2.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(-5,\\frac{2.6}{65}) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,0.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9) & ≈P(Z<\\frac{-4.9-(-5)}{\\sqrt{0.04}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.5) \\\\  & =Φ(0.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9)=Φ(0.5).\n$$" "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=65,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 0.1+(-6)\\cdot 0.4+(-4)\\cdot 0.4+(-2)\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =-0.8-2.4-1.6-0.2 \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =((-8)^{2}\\cdot 0.1+(-6)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-(-5)^{2} \\\\  & =(6.4+14.4+6.4+0.4)-25 \\\\  & =2.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\,\\left(-5,\\frac{2.6}{65}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,0.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9) & ≈P\\left(Z<\\frac{-4.9-(-5)}{\\sqrt{0.04}}\\right) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.5) \\\\  & =Φ(0.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9)=Φ(0.5).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484263 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =4\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =4⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8⟨1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =(4⟨2t,\\,1,\\,0⟩)\\times (8⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩) \\\\  & =32\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =32\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-32\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=32.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{(4\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{64\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨8t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =4\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =4\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =4⟨2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8⟨1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =(4⟨2t,\\,1,\\,0⟩)\\times (8⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩) \\\\  & =32\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =32\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =-32\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=32.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{(4\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{64\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484267 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)-16({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}(\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}+16)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{a}_{n}(\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}+16)=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$" "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\right)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\right)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)-16\\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\left(\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}+16\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{a}_{n}\\left(\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}+16\\right)=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484272 :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3(x-1)}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3x-3}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}+(-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}){|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+3-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3(x-1)}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3x-3}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}\\right){|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+3-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484275 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =2‖⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩‖ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩) \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =2‖⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩‖ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)+{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484282 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16{k}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}k-\\frac{1}{9} &= -(16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference  formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9} &= (4k)^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= (4k)^{2}-2\\cdot (4k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})+{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= {(4k-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9}) &= -{(4k-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16{k}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}k-\\frac{1}{9} &= -\\left(16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference  formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9} &= (4k)^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= (4k)^{2}-2\\cdot \\left(4k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= {\\left(4k-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\left(16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9}\\right) &= -{\\left(4k-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484295 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25} &= -(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25} &= (2x)^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= (2x)^{2}+2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2x)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= {(2x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25}) &= -{(2x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25} &= -\\left(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25} &= (2x)^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= (2x)^{2}+2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2x\\right)+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ &= {\\left(2x+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\left(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25}\\right) &= -{\\left(2x+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484298 :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2x}{27}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{27}(9-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{27}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{1}{27}(9{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{4}}{54}|}_{0}^{3}+{\\frac{1}{27}(3{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{2}-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2x}{27}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{27}(9-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{27}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{1}{27}(9{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{4}}{54}|}_{0}^{3}+{\\frac{1}{27}\\left(3{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{2}-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484383 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(14,\\frac{7}{2})$ and the line $3 x + 2 y=-42.$" "Find the shortest distance between the point $\\left(14,\\frac{7}{2}\\right)$ and the line $3 x + 2 y=-42.$"] [:db/cas 17592186484383 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 x + 2 y & =-42 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/bb/bbd49aa51f1721cb274e2fe4451aaec3319884eff891165fe0a9e941b04b4a70.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21$ and passes through the point $(14,\\frac{7}{2}).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-(14)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2 x}{3} -\\frac{35}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21 \\\\ y=\\frac{2 x}{3} -\\frac{35}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21 & =\\frac{2 x}{3} -\\frac{35}{6} \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{13}{6}\\right)x & =\\frac{91}{6} \\\\ x & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-7$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-\\frac{21}{2}.$ So, our solution is $x=-7$ and $y=-\\frac{21}{2}.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(-7,-\\frac{21}{2}).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(14,\\frac{7}{2})$ and $(-7,-\\frac{21}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{(-7-(14))^2+(-\\frac{21}{2}-\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right))^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{441+196} \\\\ & =7 \\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(14,\\frac{7}{2})$ and the line $3 x + 2 y=-42$ is $7 \\sqrt{13}.$" "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 x + 2 y & =-42 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/bb/bbd49aa51f1721cb274e2fe4451aaec3319884eff891165fe0a9e941b04b4a70.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21$ and passes through the point $\\left(14,\\frac{7}{2}\\right).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-(14)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2 x}{3} -\\frac{35}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21 \\\\ y=\\frac{2 x}{3} -\\frac{35}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21 & =\\frac{2 x}{3} -\\frac{35}{6} \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{13}{6}\\right)x & =\\frac{91}{6} \\\\ x & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-7$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-\\frac{21}{2}.$ So, our solution is $x=-7$ and $y=-\\frac{21}{2}.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $\\left(-7,-\\frac{21}{2}\\right).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $\\left(14,\\frac{7}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(-7,-\\frac{21}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{(-7-(14))^2+\\left(-\\frac{21}{2}-\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\right)^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{441+196} \\\\ & =7 \\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $\\left(14,\\frac{7}{2}\\right)$ and the line $3 x + 2 y=-42$ is $7 \\sqrt{13}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186484409 :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-4{x}^{-1/2}\\right)) \\\\  & =3\\left(8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7}}{4}+1-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot 4{x}^{-3/2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right)) \\\\  & =3\\left(7\\cdot 2{x}^{6}+0+\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\left(14{x}^{6}-3{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\left(14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(3\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(3\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-4{x}^{-1/2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =3\\left(8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7}}{4}+1-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot 4{x}^{-3/2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right)) \\\\  & =3\\left(7\\cdot 2{x}^{6}+0+\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\left(14{x}^{6}-3{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\left(14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484412 :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-4{x}^{-1/2}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\left(8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7}}{4}+1-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot 4{x}^{-3/2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\left(7\\cdot 2{x}^{6}+0+\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(14{x}^{6}-3{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(2\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-4{x}^{-1/2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7}}{4}+1-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot 4{x}^{-3/2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\left(7\\cdot 2{x}^{6}+0+\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(14{x}^{6}-3{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484415 :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-4{x}^{-1/2}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\left(8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7}}{4}+1-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot 4{x}^{-3/2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\left(7\\cdot 2{x}^{6}+0+\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(14{x}^{6}-3{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{4}+x-4{x}^{-1/2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7}}{4}+1-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot 4{x}^{-3/2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\left(2{x}^{7}+1+2{x}^{-3/2}\\right)) \\\\  & =4\\left(7\\cdot 2{x}^{6}+0+\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(14{x}^{6}-3{x}^{-5/2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(14{x}^{6}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484773 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (-9-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=-9-9z.$ Then differentiating with respect to $z$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=-9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}z=-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $z$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-9-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\frac{1}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}(-9-9z)^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{-9-9z}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{-9-9z}+C] & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[\\sqrt{-9-9z}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[(-9-9z)^{1/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(-9-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(-9-9z)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (-9-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =(-9-9z)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-9-9z}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (-9-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=-9-9z.$ Then differentiating with respect to $z$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=-9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}z=-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $z$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-9-9z)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\frac{1}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}(-9-9z)^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{-9-9z}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}\\left[-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{-9-9z}+C\\right] & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[\\sqrt{-9-9z}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}[(-9-9z)^{1/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(-9-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}z}(-9-9z)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (-9-9z)^{-1/2}\\cdot (-9) \\\\  & =(-9-9z)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-9-9z}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484781 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (5x-50)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=5x-50.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5x-50)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(5x-50)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x-50)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(5x-50)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-50)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5x-50)^{1/2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(5x-50)^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5x-50}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (5x-50)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=5x-50.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5x-50)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(5x-50)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}+C\\right] & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(5x-50)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x-50)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(5x-50)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-50)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5x-50)^{1/2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(5x-50)^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5x-50}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484799 :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$" "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484800 :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$$" "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484801 :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{3}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{3})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}.$$" "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ 