\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484803 :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3})\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}.$$" "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by $f'(0)$.\n\nWe calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$f=\\cos u,\\qquad u=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=-\\sin u,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\cos x.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule:\n\n$$f'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\sin\\left(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\cos x.$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives $$\n\\displaystyle f'(0)=-\\sin\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, use the point-slope equation:\n\n$$y-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)(x-0),\\qquad y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3} x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}.$$"] [:db/cas 17592186484814 :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩" "T(s)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left\\langle 1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186484837 :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2})" "\\left(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186484851 :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4})" "\\left(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186484865 :answer/value "(1,\\frac{5}{2})" "\\left(1,\\frac{5}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186484919 :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3})" "\\left(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186484922 :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\sqrt{3+s},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3-s}⟩" "T(s)=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\left\\langle \\sqrt{3+s},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3-s}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186484949 :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},-1,\\frac{3}{2})" "\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},-1,\\frac{3}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186484975 :answer/value "T(s)=⟨\\sqrt{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩" "T(s)=\\left\\langle \\sqrt{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186485061 :answer/value "T(s)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩" "T(s)=\\left\\langle \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186485150 :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{9}{4})" "\\left(-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{9}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186485156 :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\sqrt{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩" "T(s)=\\frac{1}{3}\\left\\langle \\sqrt{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186485203 :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{1}{4}⟨\\sqrt{7},\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩" "T(s)=\\frac{1}{4}\\left\\langle \\sqrt{7},\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186485207 :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2})" "\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186485274 :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{11}{5})" "\\left(\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{11}{5}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186485498 :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that the first box is selected, and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected marble is black. Then, we require $P(F|B),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(F|B)=\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|F)P(F)+P(B|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${F}^{′}$ is the event that the second box is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first box contains two black and four blue marbles, and the second box contains one black and five blue marbles, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(B|{F}^{′})=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\n- Since the box is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=P({F}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|F)P(F)+P(B|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $F$ be the event that the first box is selected, and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected marble is black. Then, we require $P(F|B),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(F|B)=\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|F)P(F)+P(B|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${F}^{′}$ is the event that the second box is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first box contains two black and four blue marbles, and the second box contains one black and five blue marbles, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(B|{F}^{′})=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\n- Since the box is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=P({F}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|F)P(F)+P(B|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485503 :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=3t,$ we have that $t=\\frac{s}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{s\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parameterized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{s\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\sqrt{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, since $s=3t,$ we have that $t=\\frac{s}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{s\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parameterized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{s\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left\\langle \\sqrt{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right)\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485525 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-x-y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,-2,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,-2,3)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨-1,-2,3⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 12-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩ \\\\ 12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-1,-1,2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-1,-1,2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩ \\\\ 12 & =⟨-1,-1,2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩ \\\\ 12 & =1+2+6{c}_{3} \\\\ 6{c}_{3} & =9 \\\\ {c}_{3} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{(-1)} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-2)}=\\frac{z-0}{3} \\\\ -x & =-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-2t,\\,z=3t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-t,\\,{c}_{2}=-2t,\\,{c}_{3}=3t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation ${c}_{3}=\\frac{3}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3t & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1,\\frac{3}{2}).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-x-y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,-2,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,-2,3)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨-1,-2,3⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 12-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩ \\\\ 12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-1,-1,2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-1,-1,2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩ \\\\ 12 & =⟨-1,-1,2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩ \\\\ 12 & =1+2+6{c}_{3} \\\\ 6{c}_{3} & =9 \\\\ {c}_{3} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{(-1)} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-2)}=\\frac{z-0}{3} \\\\ -x & =-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-2t,\\,z=3t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-t,\\,{c}_{2}=-2t,\\,{c}_{3}=3t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation ${c}_{3}=\\frac{3}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3t & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},-1,\\frac{3}{2}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186485536 :question/problem "The equivalent radical expression for ${({\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{1/4})}^{7/3}$ is" "The equivalent radical expression for ${\\left({\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{1/4}\\right)}^{7/3}$ is"] [:db/cas 17592186485536 :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the power rule and then we express the resulting fractional power as a radical: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{({\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{1/4})}^{7/3} &= {\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{(1/4)(7/3)} \\\\\n&= {\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{7/12} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt[12]{{\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{7}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we apply the power rule and then we express the resulting fractional power as a radical: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{\\left({\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{1/4}\\right)}^{7/3} &= {\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{(1/4)(7/3)} \\\\\n&= {\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{7/12} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt[12]{{\\left(\\frac{2}{u}\\right)}^{7}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485542 :question/problem "Given the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=⟨\\frac{4}{3}(1+t)^{3/2},\\,t,\\,\\frac{4}{3}(1-t)^{3/2}⟩,\n$$ $-1\\le t\\le 1,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=3t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" "Given the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{4}{3}(1+t)^{3/2},\\,t,\\,\\frac{4}{3}(1-t)^{3/2}\\right\\rangle ,\n$$ $-1\\le t\\le 1,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=3t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$"] [:db/cas 17592186485542 :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=3t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{3}{(1+\\frac{s}{3})}^{3/2},\\,\\frac{s}{3},\\,\\frac{4}{3}{(1-\\frac{s}{3})}^{3/2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parameterized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4}{3}{(1+\\frac{s}{3})}^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4}{3}{(1+\\frac{s}{3})}^{3/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{s}{3}},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{s}{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\sqrt{3+s},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3-s}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, since $s=3t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{4}{3}{\\left(1+\\frac{s}{3}\\right)}^{3/2},\\,\\frac{s}{3},\\,\\frac{4}{3}{\\left(1-\\frac{s}{3}\\right)}^{3/2}\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parameterized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}{\\left(1+\\frac{s}{3}\\right)}^{3/2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{s}{3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}{\\left(1+\\frac{s}{3}\\right)}^{3/2}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{s}{3}},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{s}{3}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\left\\langle \\sqrt{3+s},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3-s}\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485562 :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the second bin is selected, and let $G$ be the event that a randomly selected chip is green. Then, we require $P(S|G),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(S|G)=\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G)}=\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G|S)P(S)+P(G|{S}^{′})P({S}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${S}^{′}$ is the event that the first bin is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first bin contains four green and two yellow chips, and the second bin contains three green and one yellow chip, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|S)=\\frac{3}{4},\\,P(G|{S}^{′})=\\frac{4}{6}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the bin is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(S)=P({S}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S|G) & =\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G|S)P(S)+P(G|{S}^{′})P({S}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}{(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{17}{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $S$ be the event that the second bin is selected, and let $G$ be the event that a randomly selected chip is green. Then, we require $P(S|G),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(S|G)=\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G)}=\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G|S)P(S)+P(G|{S}^{′})P({S}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${S}^{′}$ is the event that the first bin is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first bin contains four green and two yellow chips, and the second bin contains three green and one yellow chip, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|S)=\\frac{3}{4},\\,P(G|{S}^{′})=\\frac{4}{6}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the bin is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(S)=P({S}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S|G) & =\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G|S)P(S)+P(G|{S}^{′})P({S}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{17}{12}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485570 :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that the first box is selected, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected marble is red. Then, we require $P(F|R),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(F|R)=\\frac{P(R|F)P(F)}{P(R)}=\\frac{P(R|F)P(F)}{P(R|F)P(F)+P(R|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${F}^{′}$ is the event that the second box is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first box contains two black and three red marbles, and the second box contains four black and three red marbles, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|F)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P(R|{F}^{′})=\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\n- Since the box is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=P({F}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|R) & =\\frac{P(R|F)P(F)}{P(R|F)P(F)+P(R|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}{(\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{3}{7})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $F$ be the event that the first box is selected, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected marble is red. Then, we require $P(F|R),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(F|R)=\\frac{P(R|F)P(F)}{P(R)}=\\frac{P(R|F)P(F)}{P(R|F)P(F)+P(R|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${F}^{′}$ is the event that the second box is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first box contains two black and three red marbles, and the second box contains four black and three red marbles, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|F)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P(R|{F}^{′})=\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\n- Since the box is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=P({F}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|R) & =\\frac{P(R|F)P(F)}{P(R|F)P(F)+P(R|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{3}{7}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485572 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=5{y}^{2}-2x$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3)-f(0,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3⟩-⟨0,2⟩) \\\\ (5(3)^{2}-2(2))-(5(2)^{2}-2(0)) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2,10y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =-4+10{c}_{2} \\\\ 10{c}_{2} & =25 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3-2} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & =2y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2{c}_{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2},$ we obtain that ${c}_{1}=2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}-4=1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(1,\\frac{5}{2}).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=5{y}^{2}-2x$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3)-f(0,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3⟩-⟨0,2⟩) \\\\ (5(3)^{2}-2(2))-(5(2)^{2}-2(0)) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2,10y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =-4+10{c}_{2} \\\\ 10{c}_{2} & =25 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3-2} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & =2y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2{c}_{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2},$ we obtain that ${c}_{1}=2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}-4=1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $\\left(1,\\frac{5}{2}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186485667 :answer/value "\\ln(\\frac{19}{2}|x|+K)" "\\ln\\left(\\frac{19}{2}|x|+K\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186485678 :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{5}{2x})}^{2}?$" "What is the antiderivative of ${\\left(1+\\frac{5}{2x}\\right)}^{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186485678 :question/worked-solution "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{5}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{25}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{25}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+5\\ln|x|+\\frac{25{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+5\\ln|x|-\\frac{25}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=5\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $5\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{5}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5\\ln|x|+5\\ln K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\left(1+\\frac{5}{2x}\\right)}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(1+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{25}{4{x}^{2}}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{25}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+5\\ln|x|+\\frac{25{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+5\\ln|x|-\\frac{25}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=5\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $5\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\left(1+\\frac{5}{2x}\\right)}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5\\ln|x|+5\\ln K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186485683 :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{31}{2x})}^{2}?$" "What is the antiderivative of ${\\left(1+\\frac{31}{2x}\\right)}^{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186485683 :question/worked-solution "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{31}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{31}{x}+\\frac{961}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+31\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{961}{4}\\