(12-5x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 18{u}^{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\int {u}^{5}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{u}^{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}(12-5x)^{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{3}{5}(12-5x)^{6}+C] & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{3}{5}(12-5x)^{6}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}(12-5x)^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}(12-5x)^{5}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =18(12-5x)^{5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u=12-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 18(12-5x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 18{u}^{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\int {u}^{5}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{u}^{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}(12-5x)^{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[-\\frac{3}{5}(12-5x)^{6}+C\\right] & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[\\frac{3}{5}(12-5x)^{6}\\right]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}(12-5x)^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5}(12-5x)^{5}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =18(12-5x)^{5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486356 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-18{x}^{2}}{12p{x}^{2}-3x+9} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{18{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{12p{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-18)}{(12p-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-18}{12p-0+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2p} & =-2 \\\\ 4p & =3 \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-18{x}^{2}}{12p{x}^{2}-3x+9} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{18{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{12p{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-18\\right)}{\\left(12p-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-18}{12p-0+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2p} & =-2 \\\\ 4p & =3 \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186486357 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{7{n}^{4}+3}{4n-5{n}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-5{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{7{n}^{4}+3}{4n-5{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{4n}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{5{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7+\\frac{3}{{n}^{4}})}{\\frac{4}{{n}^{3}}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{7+0}{0-5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $-\\frac{7}{5}.$" "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{7{n}^{4}+3}{4n-5{n}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-5{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{7{n}^{4}+3}{4n-5{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{n}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4n}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{5{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(7+\\frac{3}{{n}^{4}}\\right)}{\\frac{4}{{n}^{3}}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{7+0}{0-5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $-\\frac{7}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186486359 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(4n-1)(4n+1)}{n(7-16{n}^{2})}=\\frac{16{n}^{2}-1}{7n-16{n}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{16{n}^{2}-1}{7n-16{n}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-16{n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{16{n}^{2}-1}{7n-16{n}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{