he equation in standard form by $I(x)=e^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+1y) & =e^{x}\\cdot 5 \\\\ e^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+e^{x}y & =5 e^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{x}) & =5 e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5 e^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ ye^{x} & =5\\left(1 e^{x}\\right)+C \\\\ ye^{x} & =5 e^{x}+C \\\\ y & =\\left(C + 5 e^{x}\\right) e^{- x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =\\frac{(5 e^{(0)})+C}{(e^{(0)})} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{5+C}{1} \\\\ C & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5 - 6 e^{- x}.\n$$" "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=5.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =e^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =e^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=e^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e^{x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+1y\\right) & =e^{x}\\cdot 5 \\\\ e^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+e^{x}y & =5 e^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{x}) & =5 e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5 e^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ ye^{x} & =5\\left(1 e^{x}\\right)+C \\\\ ye^{x} & =5 e^{x}+C \\\\ y & =\\left(C + 5 e^{x}\\right) e^{- x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =\\frac{(5 e^{(0)})+C}{(e^{(0)})} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{5+C}{1} \\\\ C & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5 - 6 e^{- x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186495673 :answer/value "(-1,\\frac{5}{2})" "\\left(-1,\\frac{5}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186495786 :answer/value "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$" "$\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186495826 :answer/value "$(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ and $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$" "$\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186495852 :answer/value "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ only" "$\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186495860 :answer/value "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$" "$\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186495958 :answer/value "$(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ only" "$\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186496060 :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2})" "\\left(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186496226 :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It so happens that the second equation is already solved for $u:$\n\n$$\nu=-v-2\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $u$ with $-v-2$ in the first equation and solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u-6v & =-3 \\\\ 4(-v-2)-6v & =-3 \\\\ -4v-8-6v & =-3 \\\\ -10v & =5 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into $u=-v-2,$ then we can find the value of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-v-2 \\\\ u & =-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-2 \\\\ u & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$" "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It so happens that the second equation is already solved for $u:$\n\n$$\nu=-v-2\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $u$ with $-v-2$ in the first equation and solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u-6v & =-3 \\\\ 4(-v-2)-6v & =-3 \\\\ -4v-8-6v & =-3 \\\\ -10v & =5 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into $u=-v-2,$ then we can find the value of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-v-2 \\\\ u & =-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-2 \\\\ u & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $\\left(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186496228 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the waiting time, and let $Y$ denote the conversation time. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X+Y>30).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(15,{3}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(10,{4}^{2})$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(15+10,{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(25,25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =25$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X+Y>30),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>30) & =P(Z>\\frac{30-25}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1)≈0.8413.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>30) & =1-Φ(1) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8413 \\\\  & ≈0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $X$ denote the waiting time, and let $Y$ denote the conversation time. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X+Y>30).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(15,{3}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(10,{4}^{2})$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(15+10,{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(25,25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =25$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X+Y>30),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>30) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{30-25}{5}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1)≈0.8413.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>30) & =1-Φ(1) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8413 \\\\  & ≈0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496233 :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $-1$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3u+z &=-2 \\\\ (\\,3u-5z &=-17\\,)\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3u+z &=-2 \\\\ -3u+5z &=17\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $u,$ and solve the resulting equation for $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{3u}+z & =-2 \\\\ -\\cancel{3u}+5z & =17 \\\\ \\hline (z+5z) & =(-2+17) \\\\ 6z & =15 \\\\ z & =\\frac{15}{6} \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $z=\\frac{5}{2}.$ However, we still need to find $u.$ To do this, we substitute $z=\\frac{5}{2}$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3u+z & =-2 \\\\ 3u+\\frac{5}{2} & =-2 \\\\ 3u & =-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ u & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,z=\\frac{5}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$" "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the second equation by $-1$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3u+z &=-2 \\\\ (\\,3u-5z &=-17\\,)\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3u+z &=-2 \\\\ -3u+5z &=17\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together, *eliminate* the variable $u,$ and solve the resulting equation for $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{3u}+z & =-2 \\\\ -\\cancel{3u}+5z & =17 \\\\ \\hline (z+5z) & =(-2+17) \\\\ 6z & =15 \\\\ z & =\\frac{15}{6} \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $z=\\frac{5}{2}.$ However, we still need to find $u.$ To do this, we substitute $z=\\frac{5}{2}$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3u+z & =-2 \\\\ 3u+\\frac{5}{2} & =-2 \\\\ 3u & =-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ u & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,z=\\frac{5}{2},$ which can be expressed as $\\left(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186496238 :question/worked-solution "![](images/9a/9ac139d8823f326f32869f308cb2d04cdc9e0d86dba7d4a11b66955afd665da3.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k{x}^{2}(x-5)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k{x}^{2}(x-5).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k{x}^{2}(x-5).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{3})^{2} & =k(\\frac{(0)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5(0)^{3}}{3})+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=0\\,m/s$ when $x=6\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =k(\\frac{(6)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5(6)^{3}}{3})+6 \\\\ 0 & =k(324-360)+6 \\\\ 0 & =-36k+6 \\\\ 36k & =6 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "![](images/9a/9ac139d8823f326f32869f308cb2d04cdc9e0d86dba7d4a11b66955afd665da3.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k{x}^{2}(x-5)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k{x}^{2}(x-5).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right)=k{x}^{2}(x-5).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right)\\text{d}x=\\int k({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{3})^{2} & =k\\left(\\frac{(0)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5(0)^{3}}{3}\\right)+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)+6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=0\\,m/s$ when $x=6\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)+6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =k\\left(\\frac{(6)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5(6)^{3}}{3}\\right)+6 \\\\ 0 & =k(324-360)+6 \\\\ 0 & =-36k+6 \\\\ 36k & =6 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496244 :question/worked-solution "![](images/60/602ef5480fe59ccf921d17a19d10848a1a8a04a1fc3fb3181b8e9b17ebe81fad.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(7-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(7-x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(7-x) & =0 \\\\ 7-x & =0 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=7\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,7)$ | $7$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $6$ |  | $8$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $k>0$ | $0$ | $-k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=7\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(7-x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(7-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =k(7(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+8.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{10})^{2} & =k(7(2)-\\frac{1}{2}{(2)}^{2})+8 \\\\ 20 & =12k+8 \\\\ 12k & =12 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =1(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =14x-{x}^{2}+16 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+14x+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+14x+16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(7)=\\sqrt{-(7)^{2}+14(7)+16}=\\sqrt{65}\\,m/s\n$$" "![](images/60/602ef5480fe59ccf921d17a19d10848a1a8a04a1fc3fb3181b8e9b17ebe81fad.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(7-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(7-x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(7-x) & =0 \\\\ 7-x & =0 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=7\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,7)$ | $7$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $6$ |  | $8$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $k>0$ | $0$ | $-k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=7\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right)=k(7-x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right)\\text{d}x=\\int k(7-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k\\left(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k\\left(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\right)+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =k\\left(7(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}\\right)+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k\\left(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\right)+8.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k\\left(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\right)+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{10})^{2} & =k\\left(7(2)-\\frac{1}{2}{(2)}^{2}\\right)+8 \\\\ 20 & =12k+8 \\\\ 12k & =12 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =1\\left(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\right)+8 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =14x-{x}^{2}+16 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+14x+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+14x+16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(7)=\\sqrt{-(7)^{2}+14(7)+16}=\\sqrt{65}\\,m/s\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496264 :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+6{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ (-t)^{2}+2(t)^{2}+6(t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 9{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$" "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+6{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ (-t)^{2}+2(t)^{2}+6(t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 9{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ z &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496267 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2})$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(7-6,1+3) \\\\  & ∼N(1,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =1$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{4}=2.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y\\le 2.1).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 2.1) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{2.1-1}{2}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.55) \\\\  & =Φ(0.55)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.55)≈0.7088.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 2.1)≈0.71.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2})$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(7-6,1+3) \\\\  & ∼N(1,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =1$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{4}=2.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y\\le 2.1).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 2.1) & =P\\left(Z\\le \\frac{2.1-1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.55) \\\\  & =Φ(0.55)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.55)≈0.7088.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 2.1)≈0.71.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496268 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the weight of the blade, and let $Y$ denote the weight of the rubber. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X+Y>128).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are  independent random variables such that $X∼N(80,31)$ and $Y∼N(45,50),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(80+45,31+50) \\\\  & ∼N(125,81).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =125$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{81}=9.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X+Y>128),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>128) & =P(Z>\\frac{128-125}{9}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.33) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.33) \\\\  & =Φ(0.33)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.33)≈0.6293.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>128) & ≈1-Φ(0.33) \\\\  & ≈1-0.6293 \\\\  & ≈0.3707.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $X$ denote the weight of the blade, and let $Y$ denote the weight of the rubber. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X+Y>128).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are  independent random variables such that $X∼N(80,31)$ and $Y∼N(45,50),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(80+45,31+50) \\\\  & ∼N(125,81).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =125$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{81}=9.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X+Y>128),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>128) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{128-125}{9}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.33) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.33) \\\\  & =Φ(0.33)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.33)≈0.6293.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>128) & ≈1-Φ(0.33) \\\\  & ≈1-0.6293 \\\\  & ≈0.3707.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496273 :question/worked-solution "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c\\times f(t)\\,dt & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =(i+k)\\times (i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+k) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{2}{3})i-(1-1)j+(\\frac{2}{3}-0)k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c\\times f(t)\\,dt & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =(i+k)\\times \\left(i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+k\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =\\left(0-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)i-(1-1)j+\\left(\\frac{2}{3}-0\\right)k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496278 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & =5{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}∼N(5{\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2},{5}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(5\\cdot 1+4,25\\cdot 0.4+6) \\\\  & ∼N(9,16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =9$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{16}=4.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y<13.4).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<13.4) & =P(Z<\\frac{13.4-9}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.1) \\\\  & =Φ(1.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.1)≈0.8643.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y<13.4)≈0.86.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & =5{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}∼N(5{\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2},{5}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(5\\cdot 1+4,25\\cdot 0.4+6) \\\\  & ∼N(9,16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =9$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{16}=4.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y<13.4).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<13.4) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{13.4-9}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.1) \\\\  & =Φ(1.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.1)≈0.8643.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y<13.4)≈0.86.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496284 :question/worked-solution "![](images/73/73da2be2514373e6d0b1052f0eca21c339b4d826d38621faf72b3e251d7d8a95.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(4-2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(4-2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(4-2x) & =0 \\\\ 4-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2k>0$ | $0$ | $-2k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(4-2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(4-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(4x-{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(4x-{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =k(4(0)-(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(4x-{x}^{2})+8.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(4x-{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =k(4(1)-{(1)}^{2})+8 \\\\ 32 & =3k+8 \\\\ 3k & =24 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8(4x-{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =32x-8{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-16{x}^{2}+64x+16 \\\\ v & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+4x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+4x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(2)=4\\sqrt{-(2)^{2}+4(2)+1}=4\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s\n$$" "![](images/73/73da2be2514373e6d0b1052f0eca21c339b4d826d38621faf72b3e251d7d8a95.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(4-2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(4-2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(4-2x) & =0 \\\\ 4-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2k>0$ | $0$ | $-2k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right)=k(4-2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\right)\\text{d}x=\\int k(4-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(4x-{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(4x-{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =k(4(0)-(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(4x-{x}^{2})+8.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(4x-{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =k(4(1)-{(1)}^{2})+8 \\\\ 32 & =3k+8 \\\\ 3k & =24 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8(4x-{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =32x-8{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-16{x}^{2}+64x+16 \\\\ v & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+4x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+4x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(2)=4\\sqrt{-(2)^{2}+4(2)+1}=4\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496301 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the weight of a watermelon, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a melon. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X>3Y)=P(X-3Y>0).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(10,{4}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(5,{2}^{2})$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X-3Y & ∼N(10-3\\cdot 5,16+{3}^{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,52).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X-3Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-5$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{52}.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X-3Y>0),$ we convert $X-3Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-3Y>0) & =P(Z>\\frac{0+5}{\\sqrt{52}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.69)≈0.7549.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-3Y>0) & ≈1-Φ(0.69) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7549 \\\\  & ≈0.2451.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the weight of a watermelon is more than $3$ times greater than the weight of a melon is $0.2451.$" "Let $X$ denote the weight of a watermelon, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a melon. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X>3Y)=P(X-3Y>0).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(10,{4}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(5,{2}^{2})$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X-3Y & ∼N(10-3\\cdot 5,16+{3}^{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,52).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X-3Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-5$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{52}.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X-3Y>0),$ we convert $X-3Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-3Y>0) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{0+5}{\\sqrt{52}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.69)≈0.7549.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X-3Y>0) & ≈1-Φ(0.69) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7549 \\\\  & ≈0.2451.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the weight of a watermelon is more than $3$ times greater than the weight of a melon is $0.2451.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496307 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{3}{\\cos }^{3}⁡ϕ]}_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{\\rho }^{5}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(-1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(32-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{32}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & ={\\left[-\\frac{1}{3}{\\cos }^{3}⁡ϕ\\right]}_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{5}{\\rho }^{5}\\right]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(-1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(32-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{32}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496308 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/09/090f210768ffd4a6be529860b54a2906e265427b7d85bcbb07e3101c801740ba.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={({\\rho }^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{4}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{6}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=\\pi /2}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =\\pi }^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{7}{\\rho }^{7}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2} \\\\  & =-(-1-0)\\cdot (2\\pi -\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}(128-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{128}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/09/090f210768ffd4a6be529860b54a2906e265427b7d85bcbb07e3101c801740ba.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={({\\rho }^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{4}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{6}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=\\pi /2}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =\\pi }^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{7}{\\rho }^{7}\\right]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2} \\\\  & =-(-1-0)\\cdot (2\\pi -\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}(128-0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{128}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496316 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-14-6,400+225) \\\\  & ∼N(-20,625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-20$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{625}=25.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-28<Y\\le 12).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-28<Y\\le 12) & =P(\\frac{-28+20}{25}<Z\\le \\frac{12+20}{25}) \\\\  & =P(-0.32\\le Z\\le 1.28) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.28)-P(Z<-0.32) \\\\  & =Φ(1.28)-Φ(-0.32)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.32) & ≈0.3745 \\\\ Φ(1.28) & ≈0.8997.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-28<Y\\le 12) & =Φ(1.28)-Φ(-0.32) \\\\  & ≈0.8997-0.3745 \\\\  & ≈0.53.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-14-6,400+225) \\\\  & ∼N(-20,625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-20$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{625}=25.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-28<Y\\le 12).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-28<Y\\le 12) & =P\\left(\\frac{-28+20}{25}<Z\\le \\frac{12+20}{25}\\right) \\\\  & =P(-0.32\\le Z\\le 1.28) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.28)-P(Z<-0.32) \\\\  & =Φ(1.28)-Φ(-0.32)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.32) & ≈0.3745 \\\\ Φ(1.28) & ≈0.8997.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-28<Y\\le 12) & =Φ(1.28)-Φ(-0.32) \\\\  & ≈0.8997-0.3745 \\\\  & ≈0.53.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496325 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the weight of a chicken, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a turkey. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(5X>Y)=P(5X-Y>0).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(1.5,{0.1}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(8,{0.4}^{2}),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5X-Y & ∼N(5\\cdot 1.5-8,{5}^{2}\\cdot {0.1}^{2}+{0.4}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-0.5,0.41).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $5X-Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-0.5$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.41}.$\n\nTo find $P(5X-Y>0),$ we convert $5X-Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5X-Y>0) & =P(Z>\\frac{0-(-0.5)}{\\sqrt{0.41}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.78) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.78) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.78)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.78)≈0.7823.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5X-Y>0) & ≈1-Φ(0.78) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7823 \\\\  & 0.2177.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $X$ denote the weight of a chicken, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a turkey. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(5X>Y)=P(5X-Y>0).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(1.5,{0.1}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(8,{0.4}^{2}),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5X-Y & ∼N(5\\cdot 1.5-8,{5}^{2}\\cdot {0.1}^{2}+{0.4}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-0.5,0.41).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $5X-Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-0.5$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.41}.$\n\nTo find $P(5X-Y>0),$ we convert $5X-Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5X-Y>0) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{0-(-0.5)}{\\sqrt{0.41}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.78) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.78) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.78)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.78)≈0.7823.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5X-Y>0) & ≈1-Φ(0.78) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7823 \\\\  & 0.2177.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496332 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2})$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(8-30,{40}^{2}+{30}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-22,2500).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-22$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{2500}=50.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-31\\le Y\\le 31).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-31\\le Y\\le 31) & =P(\\frac{-31-(-22)}{50}\\le Z\\le \\frac{31-(-22)}{50}) \\\\  & =P(-0.18\\le Z\\le 1.06) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.06)-P(Z<-0.18) \\\\  & =Φ(1.06)-Φ(-0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.18) & ≈0.4286 \\\\ Φ(1.06) & ≈0.8554\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-31\\le Y\\le 31) & =Φ(1.06)-Φ(-0.18) \\\\  & ≈0.8554-0.4286 \\\\  & ≈0.43.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2})$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(8-30,{40}^{2}+{30}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-22,2500).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =-22$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{2500}=50.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-31\\le Y\\le 31).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-31\\le Y\\le 31) & =P\\left(\\frac{-31-(-22)}{50}\\le Z\\le \\frac{31-(-22)}{50}\\right) \\\\  & =P(-0.18\\le Z\\le 1.06) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.06)-P(Z<-0.18) \\\\  & =Φ(1.06)-Φ(-0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.18) & ≈0.4286 \\\\ Φ(1.06) & ≈0.8554\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-31\\le Y\\le 31) & =Φ(1.06)-Φ(-0.18) \\\\  & ≈0.8554-0.4286 \\\\  & ≈0.43.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496445 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- 3 \\cdot 2^{x} + 5 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- 3 \\cdot 2^{x} + 5 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((5)\\int \\left(x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-3)\\int \\left(2^{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((2)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((5)\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)) + ((-3)\\left(\\frac{2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)) + ((2)\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right))+C \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}} + C + \\frac{5 x^{2}}{2} - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}} + C + \\frac{5 x^{2}}{2} - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + (\\left(- \\frac{3}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)\\int \\left(2^{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-4)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)) + (\\left(- \\frac{3}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)) + ((-4)\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{2}} + C x + D + \\frac{5 x^{3}}{6} - 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- 3 \\cdot 2^{x} + 5 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- 3 \\cdot 2^{x} + 5 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((5)\\int \\left(x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-3)\\int \\left(2^{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + \\left((2)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\left((5)\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)\\right) + \\left((-3)\\left(\\frac{2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)\\right) + \\left((2)\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\right)+C \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}} + C + \\frac{5 x^{2}}{2} - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}} + C + \\frac{5 x^{2}}{2} - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) + \\left(\\left(- \\frac{3}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)\\int \\left(2^{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) + \\left((-4)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)\\right) + \\left(\\left(- \\frac{3}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\right)\\right) + \\left((-4)\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\right)+Cx+D \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\cdot 2^{x}}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{2}} + C x + D + \\frac{5 x^{3}}{6} - 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496457 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- x + 7 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 8 e^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- x + 7 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 8 e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-1)\\int \\left(x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-8)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((7)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((-1)\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)) + ((-8)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)) + ((7)\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right))+C \\\\  & =C - \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{7 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2} + 4 e^{- 2 x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C - \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{7 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2} + 4 e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =(\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((4)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + (\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)) + ((4)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)) + (\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{x^{3}}{6} - \\frac{7 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4} - 2 e^{- 2 x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- x + 7 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 8 e^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- x + 7 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 8 e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-1)\\int \\left(x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-8)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((7)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\left((-1)\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)\\right) + \\left((-8)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)\\right) + \\left((7)\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\right)+C \\\\  & =C - \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{7 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2} + 4 e^{- 2 x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C - \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{7 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2} + 4 e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) + ((4)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + \\left(\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)\\right) + \\left((4)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)\\right) + \\left(\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\right)+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{x^{3}}{6} - \\frac{7 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{4} - 2 e^{- 2 x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496459 :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-1)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((-1)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right))+C \\\\  & =C + \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C + \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-1)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\left((-1)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)\\right)+C \\\\  & =C + \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C + \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(e^{- 2 x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)\\right)+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496461 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- 2 x^{4} - 8 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- 2 x^{4} - 8 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-8)\\int \\left(x^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-2)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-2)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((-8)\\left(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right)) + ((-2)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)) + ((-2)\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right))+C \\\\  & =C - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} - 2 x^{4} + \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} - 2 x^{4} + \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =((-2)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + (\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + (\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-2)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)) + (\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{6}}{6}\\right)) + (\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} + \\frac{2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- 2 x^{4} - 8 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- 2 x^{4} - 8 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-8)\\int \\left(x^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-2)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-2)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\left((-8)\\left(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right)\\right) + \\left((-2)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)\\right) + \\left((-2)\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\right)+C \\\\  & =C - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} - 2 x^{4} + \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} - 2 x^{4} + \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =((-2)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + \\left(\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) + \\left(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left((-2)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)\\right) + \\left(\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{6}}{6}\\right)\\right) + \\left(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\right)+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{2 x^{5}}{5} + \\frac{2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496474 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=6 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 6 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(6 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 6 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((6)\\int \\left(x^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((6)\\int \\left(e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-2)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((6)\\left(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right)) + ((6)\\left(- 2 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)) + ((-2)\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right))+C \\\\  & =C + \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} - 12 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C + \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} - 12 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =(\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-12)\\int \\left(e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((4)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)) + ((-12)\\left(- 2 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)) + ((4)\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D + \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} + 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 24 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=6 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 6 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(6 x^{3} - 2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 6 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((6)\\int \\left(x^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((6)\\int \\left(e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + \\left((-2)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\left((6)\\left(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right)\\right) + ((6)\\left(- 2 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)) + \\left((-2)\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\right)+C \\\\  & =C + \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} - 12 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C + \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + 4 \\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} - 12 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) + ((-12)\\int \\left(e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + \\left((4)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)\\right) + ((-12)\\left(- 2 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\right)) + \\left((4)\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)}\\right)\\right)+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D + \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} + 8 \\sin{\\left(\\frac{x}{2} \\right)} + 24 e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496486 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- 8 x^{4} - 6 x^{3} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- 8 x^{4} - 6 x^{3} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-6)\\int \\left(x^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-8)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((2)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =((-6)\\left(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right)) + ((-8)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)) + ((2)\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right))+C \\\\  & =C - \\frac{8 x^{5}}{5} - \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C - \\frac{8 x^{5}}{5} - \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =(\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + (\\left(- \\frac{8}{5}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((1)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)) + (\\left(- \\frac{8}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{6}}{6}\\right)) + ((1)\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right))+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{4 x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} - \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=- 8 x^{4} - 6 x^{3} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(- 8 x^{4} - 6 x^{3} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =((-6)\\int \\left(x^{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((-8)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) + ((2)\\int \\left(\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\left((-6)\\left(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right)\\right) + \\left((-8)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)\\right) + \\left((2)\\left(\\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\right)+C \\\\  & =C - \\frac{8 x^{5}}{5} - \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(C - \\frac{8 x^{5}}{5} - \\frac{3 x^{4}}{2} + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) + \\left(\\left(- \\frac{8}{5}\\right)\\int \\left(x^{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\right) + ((1)\\int \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x)+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)\\right) + \\left(\\left(- \\frac{8}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x^{6}}{6}\\right)\\right) + \\left((1)\\left(- \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\right)+Cx+D \\\\  & =C x + D - \\frac{4 x^{6}}{15} - \\frac{3 x^{5}}{10} - \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186496501 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x) &=\\frac{8 x}{5}+\\frac{6 x^{2}}{5}- \\frac{2 e^{- 4 x}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f\\prime(x)\\,\\text{d}x &=\\int (\\frac{8 x}{5}+\\frac{6 x^{2}}{5}- \\frac{2 e^{- 4 x}}{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) &=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\int (8 x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\int (6 x^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\int (- 2 e^{- 4 x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(4 x^{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(2 x^{3}\\right)+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{e^{- 4 x}}{2}\\right)+C \\\\ &=\\frac{4 x^{2}}{5}+\\frac{2 x^{3}}{5}+\\frac{e^{- 4 x}}{10}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x) &=\\frac{8 x}{5}+\\frac{6 x^{2}}{5}- \\frac{2 e^{- 4 x}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f\\prime(x)\\,\\text{d}x &=\\int \\left(\\frac{8 x}{5}+\\frac{6 x^{2}}{5}- \\frac{2 e^{- 4 x}}{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) &=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\int (8 x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\int (6 x^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\int (- 2 e^{- 4 x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(4 x^{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(2 x^{3}\\right)+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{e^{- 4 x}}{2}\\right)+C \\\\ &=\\frac{4 x^{2}}{5}+\\frac{2 x^{3}}{5}+\\frac{e^{- 4 x}}{10}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496505 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x) &=\\frac{2}{x - 2}+e^{4 x}- \\frac{2 x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f\\prime(x)\\,\\text{d}x &=\\int (\\frac{2}{x - 2}+e^{4 x}- \\frac{2 x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) &=- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int \\left(- \\frac{6}{x - 2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int (- 3 e^{4 x})\\,\\text{d}x- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int (2 x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(- 6 \\ln{\\left(\\left|{x - 2}\\right| \\right)}\\right)- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{3 e^{4 x}}{4}\\right)- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(x^{2}\\right)+C \\\\ &=2 \\ln{\\left(\\left|{x - 2}\\right| \\right)}+\\frac{e^{4 x}}{4}- \\frac{x^{2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x) &=\\frac{2}{x - 2}+e^{4 x}- \\frac{2 x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f\\prime(x)\\,\\text{d}x &=\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{x - 2}+e^{4 x}- \\frac{2 x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) &=- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int \\left(- \\frac{6}{x - 2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int (- 3 e^{4 x})\\,\\text{d}x- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int (2 x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(- 6 \\ln{\\left(\\left|{x - 2}\\right| \\right)}\\right)- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{3 e^{4 x}}{4}\\right)- \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(x^{2}\\right)+C \\\\ &=2 \\ln{\\left(\\left|{x - 2}\\right| \\right)}+\\frac{e^{4 x}}{4}- \\frac{x^{2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496549 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x) &=\\frac{8 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{3}+\\frac{2 e^{3 x}}{3}+\\frac{4 x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f\\prime(x)\\,\\text{d}x &=\\int (\\frac{8 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{3}+\\frac{2 e^{3 x}}{3}+\\frac{4 x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int (8 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)})\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int (2 e^{3 x})\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int (4 x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2 e^{3 x}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(2 x^{2}\\right)+C \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{3}+\\frac{2 e^{3 x}}{9}+\\frac{2 x^{2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x) &=\\frac{8 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{3}+\\frac{2 e^{3 x}}{3}+\\frac{4 x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f\\prime(x)\\,\\text{d}x &=\\int \\left(\\frac{8 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{3}+\\frac{2 e^{3 x}}{3}+\\frac{4 x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int (8 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)})\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int (2 e^{3 x})\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int (4 x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2 e^{3 x}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(2 x^{2}\\right)+C \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{3}+\\frac{2 e^{3 x}}{9}+\\frac{2 x^{2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496552 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x) &=- \\frac{6 x}{5}- \\frac{8 x^{2}}{5}+\\frac{2 e^{2 x}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f\\prime(x)\\,\\text{d}x &=\\int (- \\frac{6 x}{5}- \\frac{8 x^{2}}{5}+\\frac{2 e^{2 x}}{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) &=- \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\int (6 x)\\,\\text{d}x- \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\int (8 x^{2})\\,\\text{d}x- \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\int (- 2 e^{2 x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(3 x^{2}\\right)- \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{8 x^{3}}{3}\\right)- \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(- e^{2 x}\\right)+C \\\\ &=- \\frac{3 x^{2}}{5}- \\frac{8 x^{3}}{15}+\\frac{e^{2 x}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x) &=- \\frac{6 x}{5}- \\frac{8 x^{2}}{5}+\\frac{2 e^{2 x}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f\\prime(x)\\,\\text{d}x &=\\int \\left(- \\frac{6 x}{5}- \\frac{8 x^{2}}{5}+\\frac{2 e^{2 x}}{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) &=- \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\int (6 x)\\,\\text{d}x- \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\int (8 x^{2})\\,\\text{d}x- \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\int (- 2 e^{2 x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(3 x^{2}\\right)- \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{8 x^{3}}{3}\\right)- \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left(- e^{2 x}\\right)+C \\\\ &=- \\frac{3 x^{2}}{5}- \\frac{8 x^{3}}{15}+\\frac{e^{2 x}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186496776 :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,1⟩" "r=\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right\\rangle +t\\,⟨1,0,1⟩"] [:db/cas 17592186496844 :answer/value "(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{2}{x})W(x)" "\\left(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{2}{x}\\right)W(x)"] [:db/cas 17592186497020 :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{4})W(x)" "\\left(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{4}\\right)W(x)"] [:db/cas 17592186497104 :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186497123 :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" "r=\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right\\rangle +t\\,\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186497200 :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186497343 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $4x$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{x}^{2}-8}{4x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{4x}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-8}{4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{4})W(x).\n$$" "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in standard form. Since $x>0$, we can divide both sides by $4x$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{x}^{2}-8}{4x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{4x}y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-8}{4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=\\left(\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{x}{4}\\right)W(x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186497353 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${J}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{J}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}] & =2(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\frac{3}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${J}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{J}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}] & =2(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\left(\\frac{3}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186497356 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, where $f$ is $3$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $\\left[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}\\right]$, where $f$ is $3$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186497356 :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $3$-periodic on $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, we have $L=\\frac{3}{2}$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$" "Because $f$ is $3$-periodic on $\\left[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}\\right]$, we have $L=\\frac{3}{2}$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186497372 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}] & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral. We first expand the squared term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1}+x{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+({\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)-(-{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1+3{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}] & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral. We first expand the squared term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1}+x{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+({\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)-(-{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1+3{\\cos }^{2}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186497401 :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-xyz \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+yz)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xz)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(0)(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(1))\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(0))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(1,0,1)=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-xyz \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)k \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+yz\\right)\\text{ }i+\\left(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xz\\right)\\text{ }j+\\left(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xy\\right)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(0)(1)\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(1)\\right)\\text{ }j+\\left(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(0)\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(1,0,1)=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right\\rangle ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186497840 :answer/value "Y∼N(\\frac{5}{2},10)" "Y∼N\\left(\\frac{5}{2},10\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186498158 :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{4},1)$, radius: $2$" "center: $\\left(\\frac{1}{4},1\\right)$, radius: $2$"] [:db/cas 17592186498179 :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$, radius: $2$" "center: $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},1\\right)$, radius: $2$"] [:db/cas 17592186498288 :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$, radius: $4$" "center: $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},1\\right)$, radius: $4$"] [:db/cas 17592186498294 :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{4},-1)$, radius: $4$" "center: $\\left(-\\frac{1}{4},-1\\right)$, radius: $4$"] [:db/cas 17592186498300 :answer/value "Y∼N(\\frac{5}{2},1)" "Y∼N\\left(\\frac{5}{2},1\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186498328 :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)$, radius: $2$" "center: $\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},-1\\right)$, radius: $2$"] [:db/cas 17592186498490 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=1-4y$ and $Q(x,y)=2+5x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-(-4))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}9\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 5\\pi  \\\\  & =45\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=1-4y$ and $Q(x,y)=2+5x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-(-4))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}9\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 5\\pi  \\\\  & =45\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498499 :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the population mean $\\mu =15,$ the population standard deviation $\\sigma =2,$ and that the sample size $n=16.$ Therefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(15,\\frac{{2}^{2}}{16}) \\\\  & ∼N(15,0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(14.7<\\overset{―}{X}<15.3).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(14.7<\\overset{―}{X}<15.3) & =P(\\frac{14.7-15}{\\sqrt{0.25}}<Z<\\frac{15.3-15}{\\sqrt{0.25}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.6<Z<0.6) \\\\  & =Φ(0.6)-Φ(-0.6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.6) & ≈0.7257 \\\\ Φ(-0.6) & ≈0.2743.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(14.7<\\overset{―}{X}<15.3) & =Φ(0.6)-Φ(-0.6) \\\\  & ≈0.7257-0.2743 \\\\  & =0.4514.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the population mean $\\mu =15,$ the population standard deviation $\\sigma =2,$ and that the sample size $n=16.$ Therefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(15,\\frac{{2}^{2}}{16}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(15,0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(14.7<\\overset{―}{X}<15.3).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(14.7<\\overset{―}{X}<15.3) & =P\\left(\\frac{14.7-15}{\\sqrt{0.25}}<Z<\\frac{15.3-15}{\\sqrt{0.25}}\\right) \\\\  & =P(-0.6<Z<0.6) \\\\  & =Φ(0.6)-Φ(-0.6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.6) & ≈0.7257 \\\\ Φ(-0.6) & ≈0.2743.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(14.7<\\overset{―}{X}<15.3) & =Φ(0.6)-Φ(-0.6) \\\\  & ≈0.7257-0.2743 \\\\  & =0.4514.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498502 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(-1,7),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(3,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(5,9),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 7+(-3)^{2}\\cdot 2+{4}^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 7+9\\cdot 2+16\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =169.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(10,169).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(Y>22).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>22) & =P(Z>\\frac{22-10}{\\sqrt{169}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.92) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.92) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.92)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.92)≈0.8212.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>22) & =1-Φ(0.92) \\\\  & =1-0.8212 \\\\  & ≈0.1788.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(-1,7),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(3,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(5,9),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 7+(-3)^{2}\\cdot 2+{4}^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 7+9\\cdot 2+16\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =169.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(10,169).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(Y>22).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>22) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{22-10}{\\sqrt{169}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.92) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.92) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.92)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.92)≈0.8212.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>22) & =1-Φ(0.92) \\\\  & =1-0.8212 \\\\  & ≈0.1788.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498504 :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th ball, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a box. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(2.72,{0.05}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 6,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(12,{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected box of balls. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{6}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =6\\cdot 2.72+12 \\\\  & =28.32 \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =6\\cdot {0.05}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.015.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(28.32,4.015).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W<30),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<30) & =P(Z<\\frac{30-28.32}{\\sqrt{4.015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.84) \\\\  & =Φ(0.84)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.84)≈0.7995.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<30) & ≈Φ(0.84) \\\\  & ≈0.7995.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th ball, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a box. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(2.72,{0.05}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 6,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(12,{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected box of balls. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{6}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =6\\cdot 2.72+12 \\\\  & =28.32 \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =6\\cdot {0.05}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.015.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(28.32,4.015).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W<30),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<30) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{30-28.32}{\\sqrt{4.015}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.84) \\\\  & =Φ(0.84)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.84)≈0.7995.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<30) & ≈Φ(0.84) \\\\  & ≈0.7995.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498507 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(5,1),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(8,14),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(1,2),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=4{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}+6{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 8+6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =42, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}\\cdot 1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 14+{6}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 14+36\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(42,144).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|Y|>1).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|Y|>1) & =P(Y<-1)+P(Y>1) \\\\  & =P(Z<\\frac{-1-42}{\\sqrt{144}})+P(Z>\\frac{1-42}{\\sqrt{144}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-3.58)+P(Z>-3.42) \\\\  & =P(Z<-3.58)+(1-P(Z<-3.42)) \\\\  & =1+Φ(-3.58)-Φ(-3.42)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-3.58)≈0.0002,\\,Φ(-3.42)≈0.0003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|Y|>1) & =1+Φ(-3.58)-Φ(-3.42) \\\\  & ≈1+0.0002-0.0003 \\\\  & =0.9999.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(5,1),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(8,14),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(1,2),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=4{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}+6{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 8+6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =42, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}\\cdot 1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 14+{6}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 14+36\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(42,144).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|Y|>1).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|Y|>1) & =P(Y<-1)+P(Y>1) \\\\  & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{-1-42}{\\sqrt{144}}\\right)+P\\left(Z>\\frac{1-42}{\\sqrt{144}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-3.58)+P(Z>-3.42) \\\\  & =P(Z<-3.58)+(1-P(Z<-3.42)) \\\\  & =1+Φ(-3.58)-Φ(-3.42)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-3.58)≈0.0002,\\,Φ(-3.42)≈0.0003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|Y|>1) & =1+Φ(-3.58)-Φ(-3.42) \\\\  & ≈1+0.0002-0.0003 \\\\  & =0.9999.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498509 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{14}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{14} & ∼N(14\\cdot 5,\\,14\\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(14\\cdot 5,\\,14\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =N(70,56).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(70\\le Y\\le 78).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(70\\le Y\\le 78) & =P(\\frac{70-70}{\\sqrt{56}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{78-70}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & =P(0\\le Z\\le \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & =P(0\\le Z\\le 1.07) \\\\  & =Φ(1.07)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =Φ(1.07)-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.07)=0.8577.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(70\\le Y\\le 78)=Φ(1.07)-0.5=0.8577-0.5=0.3577.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{14}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{14} & ∼N(14\\cdot 5,\\,14\\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(14\\cdot 5,\\,14\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =N(70,56).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(70\\le Y\\le 78).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(70\\le Y\\le 78) & =P\\left(\\frac{70-70}{\\sqrt{56}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{78-70}{\\sqrt{56}}\\right) \\\\  & =P\\left(0\\le Z\\le \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{56}}\\right) \\\\  & =P(0\\le Z\\le 1.07) \\\\  & =Φ(1.07)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =Φ(1.07)-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.07)=0.8577.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(70\\le Y\\le 78)=Φ(1.07)-0.5=0.8577-0.5=0.3577.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498514 :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=25,\\,\\mu =-6,\\,\\sigma =15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(-6,\\frac{{15}^{2}}{25}) \\\\  & ∼N(-6,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=25,\\,\\mu =-6,\\,\\sigma =15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(-6,\\frac{{15}^{2}}{25}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(-6,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498530 :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the student's math, science, and language test results, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(70,{6}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(65,{10}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(55,{15}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the total score of a randomly selected student. So, we have\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =(70+65+55) \\\\  & =190, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={6}^{2}+{10}^{2}+{15}^{2} \\\\  & =36+100+225 \\\\  & =361.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY∼N(190,361).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y>200),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>200) & =P(Z>\\frac{200-190}{\\sqrt{361}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.53) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.53) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.53)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.53)≈0.7019.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>200) & ≈1-Φ(0.53) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7019 \\\\  & =0.2981.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the student's math, science, and language test results, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(70,{6}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(65,{10}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(55,{15}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the total score of a randomly selected student. So, we have\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =(70+65+55) \\\\  & =190, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={6}^{2}+{10}^{2}+{15}^{2} \\\\  & =36+100+225 \\\\  & =361.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY∼N(190,361).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y>200),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>200) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{200-190}{\\sqrt{361}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.53) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.53) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.53)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.53)≈0.7019.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>200) & ≈1-Φ(0.53) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7019 \\\\  & =0.2981.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498532 :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(1,{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 18,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(16\\le Y\\le 20),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ |" "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N\\left(1,{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2}\\right),\\,1\\le i\\le 18,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(16\\le Y\\le 20),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186498532 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18} & ∼N(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =N(18,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(16\\le Y\\le 20).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(16\\le Y\\le 20) & =P(\\frac{16-18}{\\sqrt{9}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{20-18}{\\sqrt{9}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{3}\\le Z\\le \\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.67\\le Z\\le 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2P(Z\\ge 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2(1-P(Z\\le 0.67)) \\\\  & =2P(Z\\le 0.67))-1 \\\\  & =2Φ(0.67)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.67)=0.7486.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(16\\le Y\\le 20)=2Φ(0.67)-1=0.4972.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18} & ∼N\\left(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =N(18,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(16\\le Y\\le 20).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(16\\le Y\\le 20) & =P\\left(\\frac{16-18}{\\sqrt{9}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{20-18}{\\sqrt{9}}\\right) \\\\  & =P\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\le Z\\le \\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.67\\le Z\\le 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2P(Z\\ge 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2(1-P(Z\\le 0.67)) \\\\  & =2P(Z\\le 0.67))-1 \\\\  & =2Φ(0.67)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.67)=0.7486.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(16\\le Y\\le 20)=2Φ(0.67)-1=0.4972.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498541 :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the volume of the chocolate, the pumpkin, and the blueberry muffins, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(57,{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(42,{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(48,{1.5}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the total volume of the randomly selected chocolate, pumpkin, and blueberry muffins after cooking. Since the chocolate muffin doubled in volume, while the pumpkin and blueberry muffins tripled in volume during cooking, we have\n\n$$\nY=2{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}+3{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =(2\\cdot 57+3\\cdot 42+3\\cdot 48) \\\\  & =384 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}+{3}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+{3}^{2}\\cdot {1.5}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4+9\\cdot 1+9\\cdot 2.25 \\\\  & =45.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY∼N(384,45.25).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y<400),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<400) & =P(Z<\\frac{400-384}{\\sqrt{45.25}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<2.38) \\\\  & =Φ(2.38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(2.38)≈0.9913.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>0) & ≈Φ(2.38) \\\\  & ≈0.9913.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the volume of the chocolate, the pumpkin, and the blueberry muffins, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(57,{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(42,{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(48,{1.5}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the total volume of the randomly selected chocolate, pumpkin, and blueberry muffins after cooking. Since the chocolate muffin doubled in volume, while the pumpkin and blueberry muffins tripled in volume during cooking, we have\n\n$$\nY=2{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}+3{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =(2\\cdot 57+3\\cdot 42+3\\cdot 48) \\\\  & =384 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}+{3}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+{3}^{2}\\cdot {1.5}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4+9\\cdot 1+9\\cdot 2.25 \\\\  & =45.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY∼N(384,45.25).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y<400),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<400) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{400-384}{\\sqrt{45.25}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<2.38) \\\\  & =Φ(2.38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(2.38)≈0.9913.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>0) & ≈Φ(2.38) \\\\  & ≈0.9913.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498549 :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th rose. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(70,{5}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 20.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected bouquet. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{20},\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =20\\cdot 70 \\\\  & =1\\,400, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =20\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\  & =500.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(1\\,400,500).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W>1\\,430),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>1\\,430) & =P(Z>\\frac{1\\,430-1\\,400}{\\sqrt{500}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.34) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.34) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.34)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.34)≈0.9099.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>1\\,430) & ≈1-Φ(1.34) \\\\  & ≈0.0901.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th rose. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(70,{5}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 20.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected bouquet. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{20},\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =20\\cdot 70 \\\\  & =1\\,400, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =20\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\  & =500.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(1\\,400,500).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W>1\\,430),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>1\\,430) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{1\\,430-1\\,400}{\\sqrt{500}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.34) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.34) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.34)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.34)≈0.9099.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>1\\,430) & ≈1-Φ(1.34) \\\\  & ≈0.0901.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498550 :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the length of the three tubes, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(6,{0.1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(6,{0.1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(6,{0.1}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the total length of the tubes. Then, we have\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =18, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={0.1}^{2}+{0.1}^{2}+{0.1}^{2} \\\\  & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY∼N(18,0.03).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y>18.2),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>18.2) & =P(Z>\\frac{18.2-18}{\\sqrt{0.03}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.15) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.15) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.15)≈0.8749.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>18.2) & ≈=1-Φ(1.15) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8749 \\\\  & =0.1251.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the length of the three tubes, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(6,{0.1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(6,{0.1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(6,{0.1}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the total length of the tubes. Then, we have\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =18, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={0.1}^{2}+{0.1}^{2}+{0.1}^{2} \\\\  & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY∼N(18,0.03).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y>18.2),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>18.2) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{18.2-18}{\\sqrt{0.03}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.15) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.15) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.15)≈0.8749.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>18.2) & ≈=1-Φ(1.15) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8749 \\\\  & =0.1251.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498555 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(7,15),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-2,5),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(12,16),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=-{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 7+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-17, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot 15+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 5+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =15+5+16 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(-17,36).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|Y|<6)=P(-6<Y<6).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-6<Y<6) & =P(\\frac{-6-(-17)}{\\sqrt{36}}<Z<\\frac{6-(-17)}{\\sqrt{36}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.83<Z<3.83) \\\\  & =P(Z<3.83)-P(Z<1.83) \\\\  & =Φ(3.83)-Φ(1.83)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(3.83)≈0.9999,\\,Φ(1.83)≈0.9664.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|Y|<6) & =Φ(3.83)-Φ(1.83) \\\\  & ≈0.9999-0.9664 \\\\  & =0.0335.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(7,15),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-2,5),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(12,16),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=-{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 7+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-17, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot 15+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 5+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =15+5+16 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(-17,36).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|Y|<6)=P(-6<Y<6).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-6<Y<6) & =P\\left(\\frac{-6-(-17)}{\\sqrt{36}}<Z<\\frac{6-(-17)}{\\sqrt{36}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(1.83<Z<3.83) \\\\  & =P(Z<3.83)-P(Z<1.83) \\\\  & =Φ(3.83)-Φ(1.83)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(3.83)≈0.9999,\\,Φ(1.83)≈0.9664.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|Y|<6) & =Φ(3.83)-Φ(1.83) \\\\  & ≈0.9999-0.9664 \\\\  & =0.0335.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498564 :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\frac{5}{2},{1}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 10,\n$$\n\nwhat is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{10}?$" "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N\\left(\\frac{5}{2},{1}^{2}\\right),\\,1\\le i\\le 10,\n$$\n\nwhat is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{10}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186498564 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{10} & ∼N(10\\cdot \\frac{5}{2},\\,10\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(10\\cdot \\frac{5}{2},\\,10\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =N(25,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{10} & ∼N\\left(10\\cdot \\frac{5}{2},\\,10\\cdot {1}^{2}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(10\\cdot \\frac{5}{2},\\,10\\cdot 1\\right) \\\\  & =N(25,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498569 :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th egg, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a carton. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(65,{5}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 12,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(50,{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected carton filled with eggs. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{12}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =12\\cdot 65+50 \\\\  & =830, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =12\\cdot {5}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =304.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(830,304).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W>850),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>850) & =P(Z>\\frac{850-830}{\\sqrt{304}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.15) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.15) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.15)≈0.8749.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>850) & ≈1-Φ(1.15) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8749 \\\\  & =0.1251.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th egg, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a carton. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(65,{5}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 12,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(50,{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected carton filled with eggs. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{12}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =12\\cdot 65+50 \\\\  & =830, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =12\\cdot {5}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =304.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(830,304).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W>850),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>850) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{850-830}{\\sqrt{304}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.15) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.15) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.15)≈0.8749.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>850) & ≈1-Φ(1.15) \\\\  & ≈1-0.8749 \\\\  & =0.1251.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498578 :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the population mean $\\mu =20,$ the population standard deviation $\\sigma =1,$ and that the sample size $n=50.$ Therefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(20,\\frac{{1}^{2}}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(20,0.02).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(19.9<\\overset{―}{X}<20.1).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(19.9<\\overset{―}{X}<20.1) & =P(\\frac{19.9-20}{\\sqrt{0.02}}<Z<\\frac{20.1-20}{\\sqrt{0.02}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.71<Z<0.71) \\\\  & =Φ(0.71)-Φ(-0.71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.71) & =0.2389 \\\\ Φ(0.71) & =0.7611.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(19.9<\\overset{―}{X}<20.1) & =Φ(0.71)-Φ(-0.71) \\\\  & ≈0.7611-0.2389 \\\\  & =0.5222.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe're told that the population mean $\\mu =20,$ the population standard deviation $\\sigma =1,$ and that the sample size $n=50.$ Therefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(20,\\frac{{1}^{2}}{50}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(20,0.02).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(19.9<\\overset{―}{X}<20.1).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(19.9<\\overset{―}{X}<20.1) & =P\\left(\\frac{19.9-20}{\\sqrt{0.02}}<Z<\\frac{20.1-20}{\\sqrt{0.02}}\\right) \\\\  & =P(-0.71<Z<0.71) \\\\  & =Φ(0.71)-Φ(-0.71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.71) & =0.2389 \\\\ Φ(0.71) & =0.7611.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(19.9<\\overset{―}{X}<20.1) & =Φ(0.71)-Φ(-0.71) \\\\  & ≈0.7611-0.2389 \\\\  & =0.5222.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498581 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{100} & ∼N(100\\cdot (-0.05),\\,100\\cdot {0.1}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(100\\cdot (-0.05),\\,100\\cdot 0.01) \\\\  & =N(-5,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(Y\\ge -4.25).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge -4.25) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{-4.25-(-5)}{\\sqrt{1}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.75) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.75) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)=0.7734.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge -4.25)=1-Φ(0.75)=0.2266.\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{100} & ∼N(100\\cdot (-0.05),\\,100\\cdot {0.1}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(100\\cdot (-0.05),\\,100\\cdot 0.01) \\\\  & =N(-5,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(Y\\ge -4.25).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge -4.25) & =P\\left(Z\\ge \\frac{-4.25-(-5)}{\\sqrt{1}}\\right) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.75) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.75) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)=0.7734.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge -4.25)=1-Φ(0.75)=0.2266.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498582 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,{5}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(2,15),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(6,{2}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =23 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+{1}^{2}\\cdot 15+{2}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 25+1\\cdot 15+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =256.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(23,256).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(Y<43).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<43) & =P(Z<\\frac{43-23}{\\sqrt{256}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.25)≈0.8944.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$P(Y<43)=0.8944.$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,{5}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(2,15),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(6,{2}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =23 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+{1}^{2}\\cdot 15+{2}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 25+1\\cdot 15+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =256.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(23,256).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(Y<43).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<43) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{43-23}{\\sqrt{256}}\\right) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.25)≈0.8944.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$P(Y<43)=0.8944.$"] [:db/cas 17592186498585 :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x-2y & =\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+({y}^{2}-2y) & =\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & ={2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and its radius is $2.$" "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x-2y & =\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+({y}^{2}-2y) & =\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ \\left({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\right)+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4} \\\\ \\left({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\right)+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot y+{1}^{2}) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & ={2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},1\\right)$ and its radius is $2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186498587 :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the weight of the chocolate cookie, the oatcake, and the macaroon, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(10,1),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(15,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(14,1.5).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the difference between the sum of $6$ times the weight of the cookie and $5$ times the weight of the oatcake, and $10$ times the weight of the macaroon. Then, we have $Y=6{X}_{1}+5{X}_{2}-10{X}_{3},$ and we want to find \n\n$$\nP(Y<0).\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =(6\\cdot 10+4\\cdot 15-10\\cdot 14) \\\\  & =-5 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={6}^{2}\\cdot 1+{5}^{2}\\cdot 2+{10}^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =36+50+150 \\\\  & =236.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY∼N(-5,236).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y<0),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<0) & =P(Z<\\frac{0-(-5)}{\\sqrt{236}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.33) \\\\  & =Φ(0.33)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.33)≈0.6293.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<0) & ≈Φ(0.33) \\\\  & ≈0.6293.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the weight of the chocolate cookie, the oatcake, and the macaroon, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(10,1),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(15,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(14,1.5).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the difference between the sum of $6$ times the weight of the cookie and $5$ times the weight of the oatcake, and $10$ times the weight of the macaroon. Then, we have $Y=6{X}_{1}+5{X}_{2}-10{X}_{3},$ and we want to find \n\n$$\nP(Y<0).\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =(6\\cdot 10+4\\cdot 15-10\\cdot 14) \\\\  & =-5 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={6}^{2}\\cdot 1+{5}^{2}\\cdot 2+{10}^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =36+50+150 \\\\  & =236.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY∼N(-5,236).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y<0),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<0) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{0-(-5)}{\\sqrt{236}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.33) \\\\  & =Φ(0.33)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.33)≈0.6293.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<0) & ≈Φ(0.33) \\\\  & ≈0.6293.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498591 :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(7,\\frac{{14}^{2}}{49}) \\\\  & ∼N(7,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(6.8<\\overset{―}{X}<7.5).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6.8<\\overset{―}{X}<7.5) & =P(\\frac{6.8-7}{\\sqrt{4}}<Z<\\frac{7.5-7}{\\sqrt{4}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.1<Z<0.25) \\\\  & =Φ(0.25)-Φ(-0.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.25)=0.5987,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.1) & =1-Φ(0.1) \\\\  & =1-0.5398 \\\\  & =0.4602.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6.8<\\overset{―}{X}<7.5) & =Φ(0.25)-Φ(-0.1) \\\\  & =0.5987-0.4602 \\\\  & =0.1385.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(7,\\frac{{14}^{2}}{49}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(7,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(6.8<\\overset{―}{X}<7.5).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6.8<\\overset{―}{X}<7.5) & =P\\left(\\frac{6.8-7}{\\sqrt{4}}<Z<\\frac{7.5-7}{\\sqrt{4}}\\right) \\\\  & =P(-0.1<Z<0.25) \\\\  & =Φ(0.25)-Φ(-0.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.25)=0.5987,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.1) & =1-Φ(0.1) \\\\  & =1-0.5398 \\\\  & =0.4602.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6.8<\\overset{―}{X}<7.5) & =Φ(0.25)-Φ(-0.1) \\\\  & =0.5987-0.4602 \\\\  & =0.1385.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186498602 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(2,2),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(1,{3}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(3,8),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=4{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =8, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}\\cdot 2+{3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2+9\\cdot 9+1\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =121.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(8,121).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(5<Y<10).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<Y<10) & =P(\\frac{5-8}{\\sqrt{121}}<Z<\\frac{10-8}{\\sqrt{121}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.27<Z<0.18) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.18)-P(Z<-0.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0.18)-Φ(-0.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.27)≈0.3936,\\,Φ(0.18)≈0.5714.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<Y<10) & =Φ(0.18)-Φ(-0.27) \\\\  & ≈0.5714-0.3936 \\\\  & =0.1778.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(2,2),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(1,{3}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(3,8),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=4{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =8, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}\\cdot 2+{3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2+9\\cdot 9+1\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =121.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(8,121).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(5<Y<10).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<Y<10) & =P\\left(\\frac{5-8}{\\sqrt{121}}<Z<\\frac{10-8}{\\sqrt{121}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.27<Z<0.18) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.18)-P(Z<-0.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0.18)-Φ(-0.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.27)≈0.3936,\\,Φ(0.18)≈0.5714.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<Y<10) & =Φ(0.18)-Φ(-0.27) \\\\  & ≈0.5714-0.3936 \\\\  & =0.1778.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499442 :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3bbe131778b11fe61a7e436d3d4729958bb207961ca9faaac91250cf4f2dde0.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=9\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=18I+500q+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(18I+500q+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 27\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =18\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+500\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+9\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{500}{9}I=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$" "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3bbe131778b11fe61a7e436d3d4729958bb207961ca9faaac91250cf4f2dde0.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=9\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=18I+500q+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(18I+500q+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\\right) \\\\ 27\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =18\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+500\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+9\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{500}{9}I=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499449 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le \\frac{2x-y}{2}\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\frac{y}{2}\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le \\frac{2x-y}{2}\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\frac{y}{2}\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\left(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3}\\right)\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499449 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=6.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |6|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,6(u+w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=6.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\left(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3}\\right)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |6|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,6(u+w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499451 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.08.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0.5,\\frac{0.08}{40}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.5,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55) & =P(\\frac{0.51-0.5}{\\sqrt{0.002}}<Z<\\frac{0.55-0.5}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.22<Z<1.12) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.12)-P(Z<0.22) \\\\  & =Φ(1.12)-Φ(0.22)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.22)=0.5871, \\\\ Φ(1.12)=0.8686.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55) & =Φ(1.12)-Φ(0.22) \\\\  & =0.8686-0.5871 \\\\  & =0.2815.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.08.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\,\\left(0.5,\\frac{0.08}{40}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.5,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55) & =P\\left(\\frac{0.51-0.5}{\\sqrt{0.002}}<Z<\\frac{0.55-0.5}{\\sqrt{0.002}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(0.22<Z<1.12) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.12)-P(Z<0.22) \\\\  & =Φ(1.12)-Φ(0.22)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.22)=0.5871, \\\\ Φ(1.12)=0.8686.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55) & =Φ(1.12)-Φ(0.22) \\\\  & =0.8686-0.5871 \\\\  & =0.2815.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499454 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.02\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.02}=50q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=10I+50q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+50q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -21\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=-10.5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.02\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.02}=50q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=10I+50q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(10I+50q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\\right) \\\\ -21\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=-10.5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499460 :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-{e}^{-4t}+3{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{7}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{24}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{42}{25}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{144}{25}\\right)}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1764+20736}{625}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{22500}{625}}=6.\n$$" "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(-{e}^{-4t}+3{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{7}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{24}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\right) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=4{e}^{-4t}-6{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{42}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+\\frac{144}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{42}{25}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{144}{25}\\right)}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1764+20736}{625}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{22500}{625}}=6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499462 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=148,\\,\\mu =98,\\,\\sigma =74.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(98,\\frac{{74}^{2}}{148}) \\\\  & ∼N(98,37).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>95).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>95) & =P(Z>\\frac{95-98}{\\sqrt{37}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{37}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.49)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-0.49) & =1-P(Z<-0.49) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.49).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.49)=0.3121.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>95) & =1-Φ(-0.49) \\\\  & =1-0.3121 \\\\  & =0.6879.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=148,\\,\\mu =98,\\,\\sigma =74.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\,\\left(98,\\frac{{74}^{2}}{148}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(98,37).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>95).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>95) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{95-98}{\\sqrt{37}}\\right) \\\\  & =P\\left(Z>-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{37}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.49)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-0.49) & =1-P(Z<-0.49) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.49).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.49)=0.3121.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>95) & =1-Φ(-0.49) \\\\  & =1-0.3121 \\\\  & =0.6879.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499467 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.0625\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0625}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9=12I+16q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(12I+16q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-4t}$ and $I={e}^{-2t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{9}{2}-6I-8q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{9}{2}-6I(0)-8q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-6\\cdot 1-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4t}-2B{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-\\frac{7}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{2} & =-4A{e}^{-4(0)}-2B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & =-4A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=A+B \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} &=-4A-2B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $B=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.0625\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0625}=16q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n9=12I+16q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(9) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(12I+16q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\\right) \\\\ 0 & =12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-4t}$ and $I={e}^{-2t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{9}{2}-6I-8q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{9}{2}-6I(0)-8q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-6\\cdot 1-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4t}-2B{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-\\frac{7}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{2} & =-4A{e}^{-4(0)}-2B{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & =-4A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=A+B \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} &=-4A-2B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $B=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499470 :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+4q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(11) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-4t}$ and $I={e}^{-t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=11-5I-4q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =11-5I(0)-4q(0) \\\\  & =11-5\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+B{e}^{-1(0)} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =-4A{e}^{-4(0)}-B{e}^{-1(0)} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =-4A-B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} &=A+B \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} &=-4A-B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and $B=\\frac{17}{6}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{17}{6}{e}^{-t}.\n$$" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I+4q\\right) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(11) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-4t}$ and $I={e}^{-t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=11-5I-4q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =11-5I(0)-4q(0) \\\\  & =11-5\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+B{e}^{-1(0)} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =-4A{e}^{-4(0)}-B{e}^{-1(0)} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =-4A-B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} &=A+B \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} &=-4A-B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and $B=\\frac{17}{6}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{17}{6}{e}^{-t}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499476 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=90,\\,\\mu =92,\\,\\sigma =30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(92,\\frac{{30}^{2}}{90}) \\\\  & ∼N(92,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95) & =P(\\frac{90-92}{\\sqrt{10}}<Z<\\frac{95-92}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}<Z<\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.63<Z<0.95) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.95)-P(Z<-0.63) \\\\  & =Φ(0.95)-Φ(-0.63)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.95) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ(0.63) & =0.7357.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, by the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.63) & =1-Φ(0.63) \\\\  & =1-0.7357 \\\\  & =0.2643.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95) & =Φ(0.95)-Φ(-0.63) \\\\  & =0.8289-0.2643 \\\\  & =0.5646.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=90,\\,\\mu =92,\\,\\sigma =30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\,\\left(92,\\frac{{30}^{2}}{90}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(92,10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95) & =P\\left(\\frac{90-92}{\\sqrt{10}}<Z<\\frac{95-92}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right) \\\\  & =P\\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}<Z<\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.63<Z<0.95) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.95)-P(Z<-0.63) \\\\  & =Φ(0.95)-Φ(-0.63)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.95) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ(0.63) & =0.7357.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, by the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.63) & =1-Φ(0.63) \\\\  & =1-0.7357 \\\\  & =0.2643.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(90<\\overset{―}{X}<95) & =Φ(0.95)-Φ(-0.63) \\\\  & =0.8289-0.2643 \\\\  & =0.5646.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499479 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{3}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{3}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{3} \\\\ v &={u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}(u-1) &=0 \\\\ v &=u.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{3}\\le v\\le {u}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a8d2549c665a918cc8d69c69136fcb49e654ebb9aa5e53611155798e791b36a9.png)\n\n   \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}v{|}_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{u}^{3})du \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{3}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{3}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{3} \\\\ v &={u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}(u-1) &=0 \\\\ v &=u.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{3}\\le v\\le {u}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a8d2549c665a918cc8d69c69136fcb49e654ebb9aa5e53611155798e791b36a9.png)\n\n   \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}v{|}_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{u}^{3})du \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499482 :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I+18q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(25) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+18I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-6t}$ and $I={e}^{-3t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+B{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=25-9I-18q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{5}{4}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =25-9I(0)-18q(0) \\\\  & =25-9\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}-18\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{5}{4}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =A{e}^{-6(0)}+B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-6A{e}^{-6t}-3B{e}^{-3t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{43}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{43}{4} & =-6A{e}^{-6(0)}-3B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ \\frac{43}{4} & =-6A-3B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} &=A+B \\\\ \\frac{43}{4} &=-6A-3B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{29}{6}$ and $B=\\frac{73}{12}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{29}{6}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{73}{12}{e}^{-3t}.\n$$" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I+18q\\right) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(25) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+18I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-6t}$ and $I={e}^{-3t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+B{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=25-9I-18q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=\\frac{5}{4}$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =25-9I(0)-18q(0) \\\\  & =25-9\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}-18\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=\\frac{5}{4}$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =A{e}^{-6(0)}+B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-6A{e}^{-6t}-3B{e}^{-3t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=\\frac{43}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{43}{4} & =-6A{e}^{-6(0)}-3B{e}^{-3(0)} \\\\ \\frac{43}{4} & =-6A-3B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} &=A+B \\\\ \\frac{43}{4} &=-6A-3B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{29}{6}$ and $B=\\frac{73}{12}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{29}{6}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{73}{12}{e}^{-3t}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499485 :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{21}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-t}$ and $I={e}^{-5t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{21}{2}-6I-5q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{21}{2}-6I(0)-5q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-6\\cdot 1-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{12}{2}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-(0)}+B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-A{e}^{-t}-5B{e}^{-5t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-A{e}^{-(0)}-5B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 2 & =-A-5B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=A+B \\\\ 2 &=-A-5B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{7}{4}$ and $B=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}.\n$$" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given relation with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I+5q\\right) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{21}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-t}$ and $I={e}^{-5t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the given relation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{21}{2}-6I-5q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{21}{2}-6I(0)-5q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-6\\cdot 1-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{12}{2}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-(0)}+B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-A{e}^{-t}-5B{e}^{-5t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =-A{e}^{-(0)}-5B{e}^{-5(0)} \\\\ 2 & =-A-5B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=A+B \\\\ 2 &=-A-5B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=\\frac{7}{4}$ and $B=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-5t}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499491 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+y){e}^{y-x}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u{e}^{v}}{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{u{e}^{v}}{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot {[{e}^{v}]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (e-1)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+y){e}^{y-x}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u{e}^{v}}{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{u{e}^{v}}{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}\\right]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot {[{e}^{v}]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(2-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (e-1)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499534 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=128,\\,\\mu =250,\\,\\sigma =64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(250,\\frac{{64}^{2}}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(250,32).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<260).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<260) & =P(Z<\\frac{260-250}{\\sqrt{32}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.77) \\\\  & =Φ(1.77)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.77)=0.9616.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<260) & =Φ(1.77) \\\\  & =0.9616.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=128,\\,\\mu =250,\\,\\sigma =64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\,\\left(250,\\frac{{64}^{2}}{128}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(250,32).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<260).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<260) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{260-250}{\\sqrt{32}}\\right) \\\\  & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.77) \\\\  & =Φ(1.77)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.77)=0.9616.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<260) & =Φ(1.77) \\\\  & =0.9616.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499537 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0.8,\\frac{0.03}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.8,0.001).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.7-0.8}{\\sqrt{0.001}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-3.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-3.16) & =1-P(Z<-3.16) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-3.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-3.16)=0.0008.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =1-Φ(-3.16) \\\\  & =1-0.0008 \\\\  & =0.9992.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{5}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\,\\left(0.8,\\frac{0.03}{30}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.8,0.001).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{0.7-0.8}{\\sqrt{0.001}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-3.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-3.16) & =1-P(Z<-3.16) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-3.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-3.16)=0.0008.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =1-Φ(-3.16) \\\\  & =1-0.0008 \\\\  & =0.9992.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499538 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=98,\\,\\mu =180,\\,\\sigma =70.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(180,\\frac{{70}^{2}}{98}) \\\\  & ∼N(180,50).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>182).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>182) & =P(Z>\\frac{182-180}{\\sqrt{50}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>0.28) & =1-P(Z<0.28) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.28).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.28)=0.6103.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>182) & =1-Φ(0.28) \\\\  & =1-0.6103 \\\\  & =0.3897.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=98,\\,\\mu =180,\\,\\sigma =70.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\,\\left(180,\\frac{{70}^{2}}{98}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(180,50).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>182).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>182) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{182-180}{\\sqrt{50}}\\right) \\\\  & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>0.28) & =1-P(Z<0.28) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.28).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.28)=0.6103.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>182) & =1-Φ(0.28) \\\\  & =1-0.6103 \\\\  & =0.3897.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499539 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $90\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=90\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=90I=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/200)}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+20q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+20{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+9(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +20(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (16\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-18\\alpha +16\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha +18\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha +16\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{18}{145},$ $\\beta =\\frac{16}{145}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{36}{145}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{32}{145}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.33,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $90\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=90\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=90I=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/200)}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=90\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+20q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+9\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+20{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+9(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +20(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (16\\alpha +18\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-18\\alpha +16\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha +18\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -18\\alpha +16\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{18}{145},$ $\\beta =\\frac{16}{145}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{18}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\right) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-5B{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{36}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{32}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{36}{145}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{32}{145}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.33,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186499542 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 60\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}+2I=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+2{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+3(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\,\\, \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-7\\alpha +9\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-9\\alpha -7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-7\\alpha +9\\beta &=30\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -9\\alpha -7\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{21}{13}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{27}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{21}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{27}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{21}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{27}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-21$ and $Q=27,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-21+27=6.\n$$" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(20\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\\right) \\\\ 60\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4I+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}+2I=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+2{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+3(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\,\\, \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-7\\alpha +9\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-9\\alpha -7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-7\\alpha +9\\beta &=30\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -9\\alpha -7\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{21}{13}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{27}{13}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{21}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{27}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{21}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{27}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-21$ and $Q=27,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-21+27=6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499558 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $25\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+5(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +\\,4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +15\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-15\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +15\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -15\\alpha -5\\beta &=\\frac{16}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{24}{125},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{72}{125}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{24}{125}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.61,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $25\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.05}=20q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=5\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+20q+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+5(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +\\,4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +15\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-15\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{16}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +15\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -15\\alpha -5\\beta &=\\frac{16}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{24}{125},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{8}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\right) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-B{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{72}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{24}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{72}{125}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{24}{125}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.61,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186499561 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=128,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+4\\cdot 0.25+6\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.4+1+0.6+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{6}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{8}^{2}\\cdot 0.25)-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.4+4+3.6+16)-16 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(4,\\frac{8}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(4,0.0625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2) & ≈-P(\\frac{3.6-4}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}<Z<\\frac{4.2-4}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}) \\\\  & ≈-P(-1.6<Z<0.8) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.8)-P(Z<-1.6) \\\\  & =Φ(0.8)-Φ(-1.6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.8)=0.7881, \\\\ Φ(-1.6)=0.0548.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2) & =Φ(0.8)-Φ(-1.6) \\\\  & =0.7881-0.0548 \\\\  & =0.7333.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=128,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+4\\cdot 0.25+6\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.4+1+0.6+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{6}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{8}^{2}\\cdot 0.25)-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.4+4+3.6+16)-16 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\,\\left(4,\\frac{8}{128}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(4,0.0625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2) & ≈-P\\left(\\frac{3.6-4}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}<Z<\\frac{4.2-4}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈-P(-1.6<Z<0.8) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.8)-P(Z<-1.6) \\\\  & =Φ(0.8)-Φ(-1.6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.8)=0.7881, \\\\ Φ(-1.6)=0.0548.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2) & =Φ(0.8)-Φ(-1.6) \\\\  & =0.7881-0.0548 \\\\  & =0.7333.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499563 :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddb44fa46fed9427723f2ff5f6fd31011271f411d5dbc58e232bc5b8bbd86607.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=32\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=32I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n10=32I+200q+8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(10) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(32I+200q+8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =32\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=0.\n$$" "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddb44fa46fed9427723f2ff5f6fd31011271f411d5dbc58e232bc5b8bbd86607.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=32\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=32I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=8\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n10=32I+200q+8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(10) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(32I+200q+8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\\right) \\\\ 0 & =32\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+200\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+25I=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186499566 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =50,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{5}{4{x}^{4}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =1.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.25,\\frac{0.1}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.25,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =P(Z<\\frac{1.2-1.25}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.12) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.12)=0.1314.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =Φ(-1.12) \\\\  & =0.1314.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =50,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{b\\to \\infty }{\\left[-\\frac{5}{4{x}^{4}}\\right]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =1.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,\\left(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\,\\left(1.25,\\frac{0.1}{50}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(1.25,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{1.2-1.25}{\\sqrt{0.002}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.12) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.12)=0.1314.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =Φ(-1.12) \\\\  & =0.1314.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186500281 :multistep/context "Mike needs to paint some walls. Each wall has an area of ${(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2}$ square meters." "Mike needs to paint some walls. Each wall has an area of ${\\left(\\frac{17}{4}\\right)}^{2}$ square meters."] [:db/cas 17592186500288 :question/worked-solution "After finishing, Mike had $(4\\,\\frac{4}{5}-3\\,\\frac{3}{10})$ liters of paint left.\n\nWriting $4\\,\\frac{4}{5}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{4\\times 5+4}{5}=\\frac{24}{5}.\n$$\n\nWriting $3\\,\\frac{3}{10}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3\\times 10+3}{10}=\\frac{33}{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{5}-\\frac{33}{10}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $10.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{24}{5}$ over a denominator of $10,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{5}=\\frac{24\\times 2}{5\\times 2}=\\frac{48}{10}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{10}-\\frac{33}{10}=\\frac{15}{10}\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{15÷5}{10÷5}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike had $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ liters of paint left." "After finishing, Mike had $\\left(4\\,\\frac{4}{5}-3\\,\\frac{3}{10}\\right)$ liters of paint left.\n\nWriting $4\\,\\frac{4}{5}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{4\\times 5+4}{5}=\\frac{24}{5}.\n$$\n\nWriting $3\\,\\frac{3}{10}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3\\times 10+3}{10}=\\frac{33}{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{5}-\\frac{33}{10}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $10.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{24}{5}$ over a denominator of $10,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{5}=\\frac{24\\times 2}{5\\times 2}=\\frac{48}{10}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{10}-\\frac{33}{10}=\\frac{15}{10}\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{15÷5}{10÷5}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike had $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ liters of paint left."] [:db/cas 17592186500293 :question/worked-solution "We can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X\\le 3) & =P(X\\le 3)-P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & =F(3)-F(1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{6}-\\frac{{3}^{3}}{1458})-(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{1458}) \\\\  & =\\frac{230}{729}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X\\le 3) & =P(X\\le 3)-P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & =F(3)-F(1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{6}-\\frac{{3}^{3}}{1458}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{1458}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{230}{729}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186500307 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the lines and curves \n\n$$\nx=3y,\\,x=7y,\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y}},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{6}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ where\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\,\\bbox[5pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the lines and curves \n\n$$\nx=3y,\\,x=7y,\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y}},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{6}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ where\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to \\left(\\,\\bbox[5pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}\\,\\right),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?"] [:db/cas 17592186500307 :question/worked-solution "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\nx=3y,\\,x=7y,\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y}},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{6}{{e}^{y}}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}=3,\\,\\frac{x}{y}=7,\\,{e}^{x+y}=2,\\,{e}^{x+y}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ and $v(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,\\frac{x}{y}\\,,\\,{e}^{x+y}).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ v &={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $x=uy.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv={e}^{uy+y}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡v=uy+y\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}$ back into $x=uy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $T$ can be given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\,\\frac{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}).\n$$" "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\nx=3y,\\,x=7y,\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y}},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{6}{{e}^{y}}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}=3,\\,\\frac{x}{y}=7,\\,{e}^{x+y}=2,\\,{e}^{x+y}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ and $v(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to \\left(\\,\\frac{x}{y}\\,,\\,{e}^{x+y}\\right).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ v &={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $x=uy.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv={e}^{uy+y}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡v=uy+y\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}$ back into $x=uy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $T$ can be given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to \\left(\\,\\frac{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186500327 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(2,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{―}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}((x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(x-2)^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}x(x-2)^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}-\\frac{4}{3}x{y}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{8}-18+\\frac{81}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ \\frac{13}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}{d}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}{d}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ d & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(2,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{―}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}((x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(x-2)^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\left[\\frac{1}{2}x(x-2)^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}-\\frac{4}{3}x{y}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{8}-18+\\frac{81}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ \\frac{13}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}{d}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}{d}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ d & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186500341 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(y+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P\\left(\\frac{1}{2},0,1\\right)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(y+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$"] [:db/cas 17592186500341 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0)\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)} & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P\\left(\\frac{1}{2},0,1\\right)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{\\left(\\frac{1}{2},0\\right)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $\\left(\\frac{1}{2},0\\right)\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{\\left(\\frac{1}{2},0\\right)} & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{\\left(\\frac{1}{2},0\\right)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{\\left(\\frac{1}{2},0\\right)}\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ z-1 & =1\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P\\left(\\frac{1}{2},0,1\\right)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186500357 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4(5y-3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=5y-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3) & =4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=20z-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=20z-3.$" "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4(5y-3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=5y-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3\\right) & =4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=20z-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=20z-3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186500362 :question/worked-solution "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRe-writing the equations \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=3x,\\,y=5x\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y=2,\\,{x}^{2}y=4,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,{x}^{2}y\\,,\\,\\frac{y}{x}).\n$$" "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRe-writing the equations \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=3x,\\,y=5x\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y=2,\\,{x}^{2}y=4,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to \\left(\\,{x}^{2}y\\,,\\,\\frac{y}{x}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186500496 :answer/value "8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}" "8{\\left(x-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}"] [:db/cas 17592186500515 :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}+{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" "\\frac{5}{2}+{\\left(x+\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186500533 :answer/value "20-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" "20-{\\left(y-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186500542 :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}" "-4{\\left(x+\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}"] [:db/cas 17592186500560 :answer/value "4{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}" "4{\\left(x+\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}"] [:db/cas 17592186500562 :answer/value "(0,\\frac{9}{7})" "\\left(0,\\frac{9}{7}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186500596 :answer/value "-\\fra