-\\frac{7}{3}).$" "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation and solve for $y.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-3y & =7 \\\\ 9(0)-3y & =7 \\\\ -3y & =7 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the $y$-intercept is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$ Thus, the line intersects the $y$-axis at $\\left(0,-\\frac{7}{3}\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186501163 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $-\\frac{1}{4}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4} & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-\\left({y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $-\\frac{1}{4}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left(y-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left(y-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4} & =-\\left({\\left(y-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{2}-{\\left(y-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186501166 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5$. So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{4}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}) & =-5({(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5$. So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5\\left({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{4}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left(y-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left(y-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\left({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\\right) & =-5\\left({\\left(y-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-5{\\left(y-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-5{\\left(y-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186501199 :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a sphere **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **square of its radius**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the square of its radius.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{4\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K.\n$$" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a sphere **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **square of its radius**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the square of its radius.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{4\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186501201 :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}\\right),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}y},\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}(y-1)}{x{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{1}^{2}\\cdot 1},\\,\\frac{{e}^{1}\\cdot (1-1)}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-e\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}\\right),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}y},\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}(y-1)}{x{y}^{2}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{1}^{2}\\cdot 1},\\,\\frac{{e}^{1}\\cdot (1-1)}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-e\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186501204 :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of the ball **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **volume**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3}\\right)}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{12\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr.\n$$" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of the ball **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **volume**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3}\\right)}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{12\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186501206 :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-3x+1) & =-({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-3x+1) & =-\\left({\\left(x-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{\\left(x-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186501218 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3$. So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}}-\\,{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2) & =-3({(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3$. So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3\\left({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}}-\\,{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left(y-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left(y-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\left({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2\\right) & =-3\\left({\\left(y-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =-3{\\left(y-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =-3{\\left(y-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186501225 :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2},\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2},\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186501235 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(x-y){e}^{x+y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(0,0) & =(0-0,0+0) \\\\  & =(0,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-2) & =(2-(-2),2+(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-1) & =(3-(-1),3+(-1)) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,1) & =(1-1,1+1) \\\\  & =(0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/0b/0b23c6463447aa1fb64bdeeca689a40711f8dad59b348bb7943c2b9d2de7e8fc.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y){e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}u{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =4({e}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(x-y){e}^{x+y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(0,0) & =(0-0,0+0) \\\\  & =(0,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-2) & =(2-(-2),2+(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-1) & =(3-(-1),3+(-1)) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,1) & =(1-1,1+1) \\\\  & =(0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/0b/0b23c6463447aa1fb64bdeeca689a40711f8dad59b348bb7943c2b9d2de7e8fc.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y){e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}u{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {\\left[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}\\right]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =4({e}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186501236 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}+7x+11)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+11\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+11\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $11$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x-11 & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}+7x+11)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+11\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+11\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}$, writing the constant term $11$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left(x+\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left(x+\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x-11 & =-\\left({\\left(x+\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{\\left(x+\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186501243 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8$. So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x) & =-8({(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8$. So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8\\left({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left(x+\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left(x+\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8\\left({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x\\right) & =-8\\left({\\left(x+\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =-8{\\left(x+\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{16}\\right) \\\\  & =-8{\\left(x+\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502072 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir minus the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of ink dye enters the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *ink dye* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+9t-6t=3(20+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *ink dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(20+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(20+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(20+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q) & =(20+t)^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (20+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(20+t)Q & =12(20+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2}) & =12(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 12(20+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(20+t)^{2} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(20+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(20+t)+\\frac{C}{(20+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of ink dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 4(20+0)+\\frac{C}{(20+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 80+\\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{20}^{2}} & =-62 \\\\ C & =-62(20)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(20+t)-\\frac{62(20)^{2}}{(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =4(20+t)-62{\\left(\\frac{20}{20+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 60+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 60+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =90 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =4(20+30)-62{\\left(\\frac{20}{20+30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =200-62{\\left(\\frac{20}{50}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈190.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir minus the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of ink dye enters the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *ink dye* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+9t-6t=3(20+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *ink dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(20+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(20+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(20+t)^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q\\right) & =(20+t)^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (20+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(20+t)Q & =12(20+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2}) & =12(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 12(20+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(20+t)^{2} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(20+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(20+t)+\\frac{C}{(20+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of ink dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 4(20+0)+\\frac{C}{(20+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 80+\\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{20}^{2}} & =-62 \\\\ C & =-62(20)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(20+t)-\\frac{62(20)^{2}}{(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =4(20+t)-62{\\left(\\frac{20}{20+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 60+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 60+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =90 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =4(20+30)-62{\\left(\\frac{20}{20+30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =200-62{\\left(\\frac{20}{50}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈190.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502074 :question/problem "A rinse tank initially holds $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved dye is pumped into the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" "A rinse tank initially holds $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved dye is pumped into the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,\\left(1-\\frac{t}{30}\\right)+\\beta {\\left(1-\\frac{t}{30}\\right)}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$"] [:db/cas 17592186502074 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water; a solution enters at $16\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+16t-24t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q) & =(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (30-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(30-t)^{-4}Q & =0.4(30-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3}) & =0.4(30-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(30-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-3} & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(30-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+C(30-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=9,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =9 \\\\ C(30-0)^{3}+0.2(30-0) & =9 \\\\ C(30)^{3}+6 & =9 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}(30-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 30\\,\\left(\\frac{30-t}{30}\\right)+3{\\left(\\frac{30-t}{30}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =6\\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+3{(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =6,\\beta =3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =6-3=3.\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water; a solution enters at $16\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+16t-24t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{24Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q\\right) & =(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (30-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(30-t)^{-4}Q & =0.4(30-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3}) & =0.4(30-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(30-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-3} & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(30-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+C(30-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=9,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =9 \\\\ C(30-0)^{3}+0.2(30-0) & =9 \\\\ C(30)^{3}+6 & =9 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}(30-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 30\\,\\left(\\frac{30-t}{30}\\right)+3{\\left(\\frac{30-t}{30}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =6\\,\\left(1-\\frac{t}{30}\\right)+3{\\left(1-\\frac{t}{30}\\right)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =6,\\beta =3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =6-3=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502075 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=99,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=99(0.45)=44.55>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=99(1-0.45)=54.45>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=99$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{99}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0025).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0025}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.00) \\\\  & =Φ(1.00).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.00) & =0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5) & =Φ(0.8413) \\\\  & =0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=99,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=99(0.45)=44.55>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=99(1-0.45)=54.45>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=99$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{99}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0025).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0025}}\\right) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.00) \\\\  & =Φ(1.00).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.00) & =0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5) & =Φ(0.8413) \\\\  & =0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502078 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=52$ from a population of size $N=120$ in which $8\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=4$ from a population of size $N=200$ in which $70\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=20$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $70\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=52$ from a population of size $N=120$ in which $8\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=4$ from a population of size $N=200$ in which $70\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=20$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $70\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic."] [:db/cas 17592186502078 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =52, \\\\ 5\\%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 120 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & <52.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =4 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =10\\,✓ \\\\ np & =4(0.7) \\\\  & =2.8 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =20 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25\\,✓ \\\\ np & =20(0.70) \\\\  & =14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20(1-0.70) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =52, \\\\ 5\\%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 120 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & <52.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =4 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =10\\,✓ \\\\ np & =4(0.7) \\\\  & =2.8 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =20 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25\\,✓ \\\\ np & =20(0.70) \\\\  & =14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20(1-0.70) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186502082 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of cleanser in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which cleanser enters the tank minus the rate at which cleanser leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleanser enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *cleanser* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $600\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of cleanser in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *cleanser* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{40}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{40}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=24.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40}+24.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleanser. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/40}+24 & =10 \\\\ A & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=24-14{e}^{-t/40}.\n$$" "Let $Q$ be the amount of cleanser in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which cleanser enters the tank minus the rate at which cleanser leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleanser enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *cleanser* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $600\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of cleanser in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *cleanser* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{40}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{40}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=24.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40}+24.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleanser. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/40}+24 & =10 \\\\ A & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=24-14{e}^{-t/40}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502085 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=65,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.35.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=65(0.35)=22.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=65(1-0.35)=42.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=65$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.35,\\frac{0.35(1-0.35)}{65}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.35,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $13$ but fewer than $26$ people bought something, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{65}<\\widehat{\\,p} & <\\frac{26}{65} \\\\ 0.2<\\widehat{\\,p} & <0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.2\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.4) & =P(\\frac{0.2-0.35}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}<Z<\\frac{0.4-0.35}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.54<Z<0.85) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.85)-P(Z<-2.54) \\\\  & =Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.4) & =Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=65,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.35.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=65(0.35)=22.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=65(1-0.35)=42.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=65$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.35,\\frac{0.35(1-0.35)}{65}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.35,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $13$ but fewer than $26$ people bought something, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{65}<\\widehat{\\,p} & <\\frac{26}{65} \\\\ 0.2<\\widehat{\\,p} & <0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.2\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.4) & =P\\left(\\frac{0.2-0.35}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}<Z<\\frac{0.4-0.35}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.54<Z<0.85) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.85)-P(Z<-2.54) \\\\  & =Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.4) & =Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502100 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that fresh water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; water enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and drains out at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n420+12t-12t=420\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{35}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{Q}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{1}{35}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{35} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{35}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{35}\\int 1\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{35}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-\\frac{t}{35}+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/35},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $14\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=14,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =14 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/35} & =14 \\\\ A & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=14{e}^{-t/35}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank $20$ minutes after the fresh water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(20)=14{e}^{-20/35}≈7.91\\,\\text{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that fresh water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; water enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and drains out at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n420+12t-12t=420\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{35}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{Q}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{1}{35}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{35} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{35}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{35}\\int 1\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{35}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-\\frac{t}{35}+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/35},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $14\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=14,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =14 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/35} & =14 \\\\ A & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=14{e}^{-t/35}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank $20$ minutes after the fresh water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(20)=14{e}^{-20/35}≈7.91\\,\\text{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186502104 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of cleaning agent in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which cleaning agents enter the tank minus the rate at which cleaning agents leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which cleaning agents enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.08\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleaning agent enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *cleaning agent* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.08\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.08\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which cleaning agents exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $400\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of cleaning agent in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *cleaning agent* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=1.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =1.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =32.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=32.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+32.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleaning agent. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+32 & =6 \\\\ A & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=32-26{e}^{-t/20}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of cleaning agent in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=32-26{e}^{-5/20}≈11.75\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "Let $Q$ be the amount of cleaning agent in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which cleaning agents enter the tank minus the rate at which cleaning agents leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which cleaning agents enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.08\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleaning agent enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *cleaning agent* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.08\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.08\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =1.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which cleaning agents exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $400\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of cleaning agent in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *cleaning agent* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=1.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =1.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =32.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=32.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+32.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleaning agent. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+32 & =6 \\\\ A & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=32-26{e}^{-t/20}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of cleaning agent in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=32-26{e}^{-5/20}≈11.75\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186502108 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of medication in the mixer at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which medication enters the mixer minus the rate at which medication leaves the mixer.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the mixer.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the medication enters the mixer at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *medication* enters the mixer is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the mixer.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the mixer remains $300\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of medication in the mixer, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *medication* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=12.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+12.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the mixer initially contains $1\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved medication. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+12 & =1 \\\\ A & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12-11{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$" "Let $Q$ be the amount of medication in the mixer at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which medication enters the mixer minus the rate at which medication leaves the mixer.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the mixer.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the medication enters the mixer at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *medication* enters the mixer is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the mixer.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the mixer remains $300\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of medication in the mixer, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *medication* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=12.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+12.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the mixer initially contains $1\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved medication. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+12 & =1 \\\\ A & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12-11{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502112 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.75.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=125(0.75)=93.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=125(1-0.75)=31.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=125$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.75,\\frac{0.75(1-0.75)}{125}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.75,0.0015).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.65<\\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.72).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.65<\\widehat{\\,p}<0.72) & =P(\\frac{0.65-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}<Z\\le \\frac{0.72-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.58<Z\\le -0.77) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.77)-P(Z\\le -2.58) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.77)-Φ(-2.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.77) & =0.2206 \\\\ Φ(-2.58) & =0.0049.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.65<\\widehat{\\,p}<0.72) & =Φ(-0.77)-Φ(-2.58) \\\\  & =0.2206-0.0049 \\\\  & =0.2157.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.75.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=125(0.75)=93.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=125(1-0.75)=31.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=125$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.75,\\frac{0.75(1-0.75)}{125}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.75,0.0015).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.65<\\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.72).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.65<\\widehat{\\,p}<0.72) & =P\\left(\\frac{0.65-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}<Z\\le \\frac{0.72-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.58<Z\\le -0.77) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.77)-P(Z\\le -2.58) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.77)-Φ(-2.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.77) & =0.2206 \\\\ Φ(-2.58) & =0.0049.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.65<\\widehat{\\,p}<0.72) & =Φ(-0.77)-Φ(-2.58) \\\\  & =0.2206-0.0049 \\\\  & =0.2157.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502114 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=200$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $80\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=30$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $70\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=200$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $80\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=30$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $70\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic."] [:db/cas 17592186502114 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =200 \\\\ 5\\%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & <200\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.8) \\\\  & =32 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.8) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =30(0.7) \\\\  & =21 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =30(1-0.7) \\\\  & =9 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =200 \\\\ 5\\%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & <200\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.8) \\\\  & =32 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.8) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =30(0.7) \\\\  & =21 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =30(1-0.7) \\\\  & =9 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186502115 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dissolved chemical in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a chemical solution containing $0.20\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved chemical enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved chemical* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.20\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.20\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =3.2\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $280\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Chemical solution enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n280+16t-16t=280\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved chemical in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dissolved chemical* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{35}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =3.2-\\frac{2Q}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{35}Q=3.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the amount of dissolved chemical in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a chemical solution containing $0.20\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved chemical enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved chemical* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.20\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.20\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =3.2\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $280\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Chemical solution enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n280+16t-16t=280\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved chemical in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dissolved chemical* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{35}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =3.2-\\frac{2Q}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{35}Q=3.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502117 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.25.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=125(0.25)=31.25>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=125(1-0.25)=93.75>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=125$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,\\frac{0.25(1-0.25)}{125}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,0.0015).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $40$ visitors in the sample rode the rollercoaster, then \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}<\\frac{40}{125}=0.32.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.32).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.32-0.25}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(1.81).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.25.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=125(0.25)=31.25>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=125(1-0.25)=93.75>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=125$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.25,\\frac{0.25(1-0.25)}{125}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,0.0015).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $40$ visitors in the sample rode the rollercoaster, then \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}<\\frac{40}{125}=0.32.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.32).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.4) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{0.32-0.25}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(1.81).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502122 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that the incoming solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\n- The reactor initially contains $520\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Solvent enters the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the reactor.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the reactor.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n520+16t-20t=(520-4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of reactant in the reactor, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{130-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{5Q}{130-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{5}{130-t}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that the incoming solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\n- The reactor initially contains $520\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Solvent enters the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the reactor.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the reactor.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n520+16t-20t=(520-4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of reactant in the reactor, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{130-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{5Q}{130-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{5}{130-t}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502123 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=50,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.2.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=50(0.2)=10>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=50(1-0.2)=40>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=50$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.2,\\frac{0.2(1-0.2)}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.2,0.0032).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.15\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.25).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.15\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.25) & =P(\\frac{0.15-0.2}{\\sqrt{0.0032}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.25-0.2}{\\sqrt{0.0032}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.88\\le Z\\le 0.88) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.88)-P(Z\\le -0.88) \\\\  & =Φ(0.88)-Φ(-0.88)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=50,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.2.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=50(0.2)=10>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=50(1-0.2)=40>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=50$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.2,\\frac{0.2(1-0.2)}{50}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.2,0.0032).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.15\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.25).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.15\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.25) & =P\\left(\\frac{0.15-0.2}{\\sqrt{0.0032}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.25-0.2}{\\sqrt{0.0032}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.88\\le Z\\le 0.88) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.88)-P(Z\\le -0.88) \\\\  & =Φ(0.88)-Φ(-0.88)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502130 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of water; clean water enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed water leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=(180+2t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{90+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{90+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(90+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $45\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=45,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =45 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(90+0)^{2}} & =45 \\\\ A & =45(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{45(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =45{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 180+2{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =45{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+60}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =45{\\left(\\frac{90}{150}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =45{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =16.20\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of water; clean water enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed water leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=(180+2t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{90+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{90+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(90+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $45\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=45,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =45 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(90+0)^{2}} & =45 \\\\ A & =45(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{45(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =45{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 180+2{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =45{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+60}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =45{\\left(\\frac{90}{150}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =45{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =16.20\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502131 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=76,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.24.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=76(0.24)=18.24>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=76(1-0.24)=57.76>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=76$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.24,\\frac{0.24(1-0.24)}{76}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.24,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.3) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.3-0.24}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.22) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.22) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.22).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.22) & =0.8888.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.3) & =1-Φ(1.22) \\\\  & =1-0.8888 \\\\  & =0.1112.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=76,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.24.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=76(0.24)=18.24>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=76(1-0.24)=57.76>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=76$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.24,\\frac{0.24(1-0.24)}{76}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.24,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.3) & =P\\left(Z>\\frac{0.3-0.24}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.22) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.22) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.22).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.22) & =0.8888.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.3) & =1-Φ(1.22) \\\\  & =1-0.8888 \\\\  & =0.1112.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502132 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ recently caught shrimps, where $55\\%$ of the shrimps in the sea weigh more than $10\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nII. A sample of $27$ companies, where $82\\%$ of the companies in the country employ more than $100$ people.\n\nIII. A sample of $100$ songs in a streaming service, given that $92\\%$ of the songs last less than $3$ minutes.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ recently caught shrimps, where $55\\%$ of the shrimps in the sea weigh more than $10\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nII. A sample of $27$ companies, where $82\\%$ of the companies in the country employ more than $100$ people.\n\nIII. A sample of $100$ songs in a streaming service, given that $92\\%$ of the songs last less than $3$ minutes.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [:db/cas 17592186502132 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.55) \\\\  & =27.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.55) \\\\  & =22.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =27(0.82) \\\\  & =22.14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =27(1-0.82) \\\\  & =4.86 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100(0.92) \\\\  & =92 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100(1-0.92) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.55) \\\\  & =27.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.55) \\\\  & =22.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =27(0.82) \\\\  & =22.14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =27(1-0.82) \\\\  & =4.86 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100(0.92) \\\\  & =92 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100(1-0.92) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186502141 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=300,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{150\\,000}{500\\,000}=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=300(0.3)=90>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=300(1-0.3)=210>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=300$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{300}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0007).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $72$ and $90$ of users in the sample have a paid account, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{72}{300}\\le \\widehat{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{90}{300} \\\\ 0.24\\le \\widehat{\\,p} & \\le 0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.24\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =P(\\frac{0.24-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.3-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.27\\le Z\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0)-P(Z\\le -2.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0)=0.5000,\\,Φ(-2.27)=0.0116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27) \\\\  & =0.5000-0.0116 \\\\  & =0.4884.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=300,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{150\\,000}{500\\,000}=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=300(0.3)=90>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=300(1-0.3)=210>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=300$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{300}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0007).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $72$ and $90$ of users in the sample have a paid account, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{72}{300}\\le \\widehat{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{90}{300} \\\\ 0.24\\le \\widehat{\\,p} & \\le 0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.24\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =P\\left(\\frac{0.24-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.3-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.27\\le Z\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0)-P(Z\\le -2.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0)=0.5000,\\,Φ(-2.27)=0.0116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27) \\\\  & =0.5000-0.0116 \\\\  & =0.4884.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502142 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of fertilizer enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *fertilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $90\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=90+18t-12t=6(15+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *fertilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(15+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|15+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(15+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(15+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q) & =(15+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (15+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(15+t)Q & =9(15+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2}) & =9(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(15+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(15+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(15+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(15+t)+\\frac{C}{(15+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of fertilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 3(15+0)+\\frac{C}{(15+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 45+\\frac{C}{(15)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{15}^{2}} & =-27 \\\\ C & =-27(15)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(15+t)-\\frac{27(15)^{2}}{(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(15+t)-27{\\left(\\frac{15}{15+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $210\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=210$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =210 \\\\ 90+18{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 90+6{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =20\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(20) & =3(15+20)-27{\\left(\\frac{15}{15+20}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =105-27{\\left(\\frac{15}{35}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈100.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of fertilizer enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *fertilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $90\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=90+18t-12t=6(15+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *fertilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(15+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|15+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(15+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(15+t)^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q\\right) & =(15+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (15+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(15+t)Q & =9(15+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2}) & =9(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(15+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(15+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(15+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(15+t)+\\frac{C}{(15+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of fertilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 3(15+0)+\\frac{C}{(15+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 45+\\frac{C}{(15)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{15}^{2}} & =-27 \\\\ C & =-27(15)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(15+t)-\\frac{27(15)^{2}}{(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(15+t)-27{\\left(\\frac{15}{15+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $210\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=210$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =210 \\\\ 90+18{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 90+6{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =20\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(20) & =3(15+20)-27{\\left(\\frac{15}{15+20}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =105-27{\\left(\\frac{15}{35}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈100.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502148 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of agent in the basin at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which agent enters the tank minus the rate at which agent leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which agent enters the basin.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the agent enters the basin at a rate of $8\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *agent* enters the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which agent exits the basin.\n\nTo find the rate at which agent leaves the basin, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the basin at time $t.$\n\nThe basin initially contains $320\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $8\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $4\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the basin after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n320+8t-4t=(320+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of agent in the basin, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{L/min},$ *agent* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{80+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{Q}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80+t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1/(80+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|} \\\\  & =80+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=80+t$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(80+t)\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80+t}Q) & =(80+t)\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (80+t)\\frac{dQ}{dt}+Q & =0.4(80+t) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(80+t)) & =0.4(80+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(80+t))\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(80+t)\\,dt \\\\ Q(80+t) & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(80+t)^{2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(80+t)+\\frac{C}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $2.2\\,\\text{kg}$ of agent. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=2.2,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =2.2 \\\\ 0.2(80+0)+\\frac{C}{80+0} & =2.2 \\\\ 16+\\frac{C}{80} & =2.2 \\\\ \\frac{C}{80} & =-13.8 \\\\ C & =-1104\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(80+t)-\\frac{1104}{80+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of agent in the basin $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(5) & =0.2(80+5)-\\frac{1104}{80+5} \\\\  & =17-\\frac{1104}{85} \\\\  & ≈4.01\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "Let $Q$ be the amount of agent in the basin at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which agent enters the tank minus the rate at which agent leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which agent enters the basin.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the agent enters the basin at a rate of $8\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *agent* enters the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which agent exits the basin.\n\nTo find the rate at which agent leaves the basin, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the basin at time $t.$\n\nThe basin initially contains $320\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $8\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $4\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the basin after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n320+8t-4t=(320+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of agent in the basin, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{L/min},$ *agent* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(4\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{80+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{Q}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80+t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1/(80+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|} \\\\  & =80+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=80+t$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(80+t)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80+t}Q\\right) & =(80+t)\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (80+t)\\frac{dQ}{dt}+Q & =0.4(80+t) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(80+t)) & =0.4(80+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(80+t))\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(80+t)\\,dt \\\\ Q(80+t) & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(80+t)^{2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(80+t)+\\frac{C}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $2.2\\,\\text{kg}$ of agent. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=2.2,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =2.2 \\\\ 0.2(80+0)+\\frac{C}{80+0} & =2.2 \\\\ 16+\\frac{C}{80} & =2.2 \\\\ \\frac{C}{80} & =-13.8 \\\\ C & =-1104\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(80+t)-\\frac{1104}{80+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of agent in the basin $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(5) & =0.2(80+5)-\\frac{1104}{80+5} \\\\  & =17-\\frac{1104}{85} \\\\  & ≈4.01\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186502151 :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=100$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=60$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=100$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=60$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic."] [:db/cas 17592186502151 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =100, \\\\ 5\\%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & <100.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =60 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,500 \\\\  & =75\\,✓ \\\\ np & =60(0.90) \\\\  & =54 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60(1-0.90) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,500 \\\\  & =75\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.9) \\\\  & =36 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =100, \\\\ 5\\%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & <100.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =60 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,500 \\\\  & =75\\,✓ \\\\ np & =60(0.90) \\\\  & =54 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60(1-0.90) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5\\%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,500 \\\\  & =75\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.9) \\\\  & =36 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186502154 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=75,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=75(0.45)=33.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=75(1-0.45)=41.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=75$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{75}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0033).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.5) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0033}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le 0.87) \\\\  & =Φ(0.87)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=75,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=75(0.45)=33.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=75(1-0.45)=41.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=75$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{75}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0033).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.5) & =P\\left(Z\\le \\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0033}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le 0.87) \\\\  & =Φ(0.87)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502157 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,200+90t-60t=30(240+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{240+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{240+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{240+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{240+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|240+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(240+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $210\\,\\text{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=210,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =210 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(240+0)^{2}} & =210 \\\\ A & =210(240)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{210(240)^{2}}{(240+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =210{\\left(\\frac{240}{240+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 30(240+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 240+{t}_{o} & =350 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =110\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(110) & =210{\\left(\\frac{240}{240+110}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =210{\\left(\\frac{24}{35}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈98.74\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,200+90t-60t=30(240+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{240+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{240+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{240+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{240+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|240+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(240+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $210\\,\\text{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=210,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =210 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(240+0)^{2}} & =210 \\\\ A & =210(240)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{210(240)^{2}}{(240+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =210{\\left(\\frac{240}{240+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 30(240+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 240+{t}_{o} & =350 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =110\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(110) & =210{\\left(\\frac{240}{240+110}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =210{\\left(\\frac{24}{35}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈98.74\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186502158 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the mass of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $18\\,\\text{g/L}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =108\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $750\\,\\text{L}$ of water.\n\n- A solution enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $6t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed water solution leaves the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $6t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n750+6t-6t=750\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the water solution at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{125}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =108-\\frac{Q}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{125}Q=108.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the mass of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $18\\,\\text{g/L}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(18\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(18\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =108\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $750\\,\\text{L}$ of water.\n\n- A solution enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $6t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed water solution leaves the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $6t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n750+6t-6t=750\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the water solution at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(6\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{125}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =108-\\frac{Q}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{125}Q=108.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502161 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.12\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.12\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.12\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =2.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $500\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Brine enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n500+20t-20t=500\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.4-\\frac{Q}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.12\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.12\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.12\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =2.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $500\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Brine enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n500+20t-20t=500\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.4-\\frac{Q}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502164 :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=350,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=350(0.7)=245>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=350(1-0.7)=105>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=350$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{350}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.0006).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $224$ apricots in the sample weigh more than $40$ g, then \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}<\\frac{224}{350}=0.64.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.64).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.64) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.64-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.0006}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-2.45) \\\\  & =Φ(-2.45).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=350,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=350(0.7)=245>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=350(1-0.7)=105>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=350$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N\\left(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N\\left(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{350}\\right) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.0006).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $224$ apricots in the sample weigh more than $40$ g, then \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}<\\frac{224}{350}=0.64.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.64).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.64) & =P\\left(Z<\\frac{0.64-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.0006}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-2.45) \\\\  & =Φ(-2.45).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502538 :answer/value "u ∈ [- \\frac{11}{2}, - \\frac{1}{2}]" "u ∈ \\left[- \\frac{11}{2}, - \\frac{1}{2}\\right]"] [:db/cas 17592186502539 :answer/value "u ∈ (- \\frac{11}{2}, - \\frac{1}{2})" "u ∈ \\left(- \\frac{11}{2}, - \\frac{1}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186502548 :answer/value "t ∈ [- \\frac{11}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}]" "t ∈ \\left[- \\frac{11}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}\\right]"] [:db/cas 17592186502583 :answer/value "t ∈ (- \\frac{11}{2}, \\frac{1}{2})" "t ∈ \\left(- \\frac{11}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186502652 :answer/value "(- \\sqrt{3}, \\frac{3}{2})" "\\left(- \\sqrt{3}, \\frac{3}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186502670 :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $- \\sqrt{3}$ and $\\frac{3}{2}$ *are* included.\n\n$$\n- \\sqrt{3}\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, \\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}- \\sqrt{3},\\frac{3}{2}{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [- \\sqrt{3},\\frac{3}{2}]\n$$" "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $- \\sqrt{3}$ and $\\frac{3}{2}$ *are* included.\n\n$$\n- \\sqrt{3}\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, \\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}- \\sqrt{3},\\frac{3}{2}{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in \\left[- \\sqrt{3},\\frac{3}{2}\\right]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592