val notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}- \\frac{2}{5},\\frac{1}{3}{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in \\left[- \\frac{2}{5},\\frac{1}{3}\\right]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502701 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x ∈ [-  \\frac{9}{2}, - \\frac{2}{3}]$?" "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x ∈ \\left[-  \\frac{9}{2}, - \\frac{2}{3}\\right]$?"] [:db/cas 17592186502872 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{7}{9}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-7/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-7/9+1}}{(-\\frac{7}{9}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/9}}{\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2/9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{7}{9}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-7/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-7/9+1}}{\\left(-\\frac{7}{9}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/9}}{\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2/9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502879 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{4}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-4/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/7+1}}{(-\\frac{4}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/7}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{4}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-4/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/7+1}}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{7}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/7}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{7}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502891 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/4+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/4}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/4+1}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/4}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502900 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/4}}={x}^{-5/4}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{5}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-5/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/4+1}}{(-\\frac{5}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/4}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-4{x}^{-1/4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{1/4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/4}}={x}^{-5/4}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{5}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-5/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/4+1}}{\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/4}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-4{x}^{-1/4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{1/4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502914 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{2}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-2/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2/7+1}}{(-\\frac{2}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/7}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}{x}^{5/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{2}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-2/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2/7+1}}{\\left(-\\frac{2}{7}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/7}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}{x}^{5/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502920 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/5+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5}}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{4/5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/5+1}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5}}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{4/5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502921 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{8/5}}={x}^{-8/5}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{8}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-8/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-8/5+1}}{(-\\frac{8}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/5}}{\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{-3/5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3{x}^{3/5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{8/5}}={x}^{-8/5}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{8}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-8/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-8/5+1}}{\\left(-\\frac{8}{5}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/5}}{\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{-3/5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3{x}^{3/5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502927 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{6/5}}={x}^{-6/5}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{6}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-6/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-6/5+1}}{(-\\frac{6}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/5}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-5{x}^{-1/5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{{x}^{1/5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{6/5}}={x}^{-6/5}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{6}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-6/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-6/5+1}}{\\left(-\\frac{6}{5}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/5}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-5{x}^{-1/5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{{x}^{1/5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186502954 :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{9/5}}={x}^{-9/5}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{9}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-9/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-9/5+1}}{(-\\frac{9}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/5}}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{-4/5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4{x}^{4/5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{9/5}}={x}^{-9/5}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{9}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-9/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-9/5+1}}{\\left(-\\frac{9}{5}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/5}}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{-4/5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4{x}^{4/5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503014 :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{3},-1)" "\\left(-\\frac{2}{3},-1\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186503024 :answer/value "$(-\\frac{2}{3},-1)$ and $(-\\frac{16}{3},4)$" "$\\left(-\\frac{2}{3},-1\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{16}{3},4\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186503123 :answer/value "$(\\frac{2}{3},7)$ and $(-\\frac{2}{3},-1)$" "$\\left(\\frac{2}{3},7\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{2}{3},-1\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186503216 :answer/value "$(\\frac{2}{3},7)$ and $(\\frac{16}{3},20)$" "$\\left(\\frac{2}{3},7\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{16}{3},20\\right)$"] [:db/cas 17592186503423 :answer/value "(-\\frac{16}{3},4)" "\\left(-\\frac{16}{3},4\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186503663 :question/worked-solution "Since our ellipse is centered at $(-1,2)$ and has semiaxes $4$ and $2,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(x+1)}{16}+\\frac{2(y-2)}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{4(y-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since our ellipse is centered at $(-1,2)$ and has semiaxes $4$ and $2,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}\\right) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(x+1)}{16}+\\frac{2(y-2)}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{4(y-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503664 :question/problem "Simplify $\\tanh \\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\tanh \\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=$\n\n| {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n| ${x}^{3}+1$ |" "Simplify $\\tanh \\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\right)$ using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\tanh \\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\right)=$\n\n| {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n| ${x}^{3}+1$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186503664 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the second definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tanh \\,(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =\\frac{{e}^{2(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}-1}{{e}^{2(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}}-1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-1}{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the second definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\tanh \\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\right) & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\right)}-1}{{e}^{2\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\right)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}}-1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-1}{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503667 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}-1}{{x}^{5}-2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})}{(1-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$" "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}-1}{{x}^{5}-2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186503669 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definition into the equation $4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})) & =-3 \\\\ 2({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & =-3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{-x} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-3) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}-2 & =-3{e}^{x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{x}-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in ${e}^{x}$ that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{x}-1)({e}^{x}+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n2{e}^{x}-1=0,\\,{e}^{x}+2=0\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}-1 & =0 &  & \\, & {e}^{x}+2 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{2} &  & \\, & {e}^{x} & =-2 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) &  & \\, & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no real values of $x$ for which ${e}^{x}=-2.$ Therefore, $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ is the only real solution to the equation." "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definition into the equation $4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\right) & =-3 \\\\ 2({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & =-3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{-x} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-3) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}-2 & =-3{e}^{x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{x}-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in ${e}^{x}$ that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{x}-1)({e}^{x}+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n2{e}^{x}-1=0,\\,{e}^{x}+2=0\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}-1 & =0 &  & \\, & {e}^{x}+2 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{2} &  & \\, & {e}^{x} & =-2 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) &  & \\, & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no real values of $x$ for which ${e}^{x}=-2.$ Therefore, $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ is the only real solution to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186503687 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right)}^{3x-3}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right)}^{3x-3} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{(x-1)+1}{x-1}\\right)}^{3x-3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x-1})}^{3(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $x-1=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right)}^{3x-3} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x-1})}^{3(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right)}^{3x-3}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right)}^{3x-3} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{(x-1)+1}{x-1}\\right)}^{3x-3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{x-1}\\right)}^{3(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $x-1=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right)}^{3x-3} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{x-1}\\right)}^{3(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}^{3n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left[{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}^{n}\\right]}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}^{n}\\right]}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503695 :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<X<1) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{28}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}([\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+(1)]-[\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}([\\frac{1}{3}+1]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<X<1) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{28}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}{\\left[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x\\right]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}\\left(\\left[\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+(1)\\right]-\\left[\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}+(-1)\\right]\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}\\left(\\left[\\frac{1}{3}+1\\right]-\\left[-\\frac{1}{3}-1\\right]\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{28}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503699 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩ & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩ & =⟨0\\,,\\,c\\,,\\,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2-2t &=0 \\\\ 4t &=c \\\\ 2+2t &=c\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $t=1.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=4.$\nThese values also satisfy the third equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2-{1}^{2}\\,,\\,2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\,,\\,2+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,2,3).$" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+{t}^{2})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩ & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2-2t,\\,4t,\\,2+2t⟩ & =⟨0\\,,\\,c\\,,\\,c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2-2t &=0 \\\\ 4t &=c \\\\ 2+2t &=c\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $t=1.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=4.$\nThese values also satisfy the third equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2-{1}^{2}\\,,\\,2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\,,\\,2+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,2,3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186503718 :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2}}-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{-2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({z}^{2}-{z}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({z}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{{z}^{4}-1}{{z}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{z}^{4}-1}{2{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2}}-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{-2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({z}^{2}-{z}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left({z}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{{z}^{4}-1}{{z}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{z}^{4}-1}{2{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503740 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}-6} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(1-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$" "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}-6} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186503745 :question/problem "Find, in ascending powers of $y,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(10y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}.$" "Find, in ascending powers of $y,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${\\left(10y+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186503745 :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=5,$ $a=10y,$ and $b=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{5}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{4}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{4},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{5}.\n$$\n\nThe $5$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,5,10,10,5,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(10y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} & =1{a}^{5}+5{a}^{4}b+10{a}^{3}{b}^{2}+10{a}^{2}{b}^{3}+5a{b}^{4}+1{b}^{5} \\\\  & =1(10y)^{5}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}+5(10y)^{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{1}+10(10y)^{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & \\,+10(10y)^{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}+5(10y)^{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4}+1(10y)^{0}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{5} \\\\  & =1(100\\,000{y}^{5})(1)+5(10\\,000{y}^{4})\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+10(1\\,000{y}^{3})\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & \\,+10(100{y}^{2})\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)+5(10y)\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)+1(1)\\left(\\frac{1}{32}\\right) \\\\  & =100\\,000{y}^{5}+\\frac{50\\,000{y}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{10\\,000{y}^{3}}{4}+\\frac{1\\,000{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{50y}{16}+\\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & =100\\,000{y}^{5}+25\\,000{y}^{4}+2\\,500{y}^{3}+125{y}^{2}+\\frac{25y}{8}+\\frac{1}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $y$, the first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{25y}{8}+125{y}^{2}.\n$$" "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=5,$ $a=10y,$ and $b=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{5}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{4}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{4},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{5}.\n$$\n\nThe $5$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,5,10,10,5,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(10y+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{5} & =1{a}^{5}+5{a}^{4}b+10{a}^{3}{b}^{2}+10{a}^{2}{b}^{3}+5a{b}^{4}+1{b}^{5} \\\\  & =1(10y)^{5}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}+5(10y)^{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{1}+10(10y)^{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & \\,+10(10y)^{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}+5(10y)^{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4}+1(10y)^{0}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{5} \\\\  & =1(100\\,000{y}^{5})(1)+5(10\\,000{y}^{4})\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+10(1\\,000{y}^{3})\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & \\,+10(100{y}^{2})\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)+5(10y)\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)+1(1)\\left(\\frac{1}{32}\\right) \\\\  & =100\\,000{y}^{5}+\\frac{50\\,000{y}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{10\\,000{y}^{3}}{4}+\\frac{1\\,000{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{50y}{16}+\\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & =100\\,000{y}^{5}+25\\,000{y}^{4}+2\\,500{y}^{3}+125{y}^{2}+\\frac{25y}{8}+\\frac{1}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $y$, the first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{25y}{8}+125{y}^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503747 :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3},3{t}^{2}+4t⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,-1⟩.$" "Given the curve $r(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3},3{t}^{2}+4t\\right\\rangle ,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,-1⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186503747 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,-1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩ & =c⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩ & =⟨c,-c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} &=c \\\\ 6t+4 &=-c\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2{t}^{2}.$ Substituting $c=2{t}^{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6t+4 & =-2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+6t+4 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}+3t+2 & =0 \\\\ (t+2)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the points corresponding to $t=-2$ and $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3}(-1)^{3},3(-1)^{2}+4(-1)⟩=⟨-\\frac{2}{3},-1⟩, \\\\ r(-2) & =⟨\\frac{2}{3}(-2)^{3},3(-2)^{2}+4(-2)⟩=⟨-\\frac{16}{3},4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(-\\frac{2}{3},-1)$ and $(-\\frac{16}{3},4).$" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+4t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,-1⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩ & =c⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},6t+4⟩ & =⟨c,-c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} &=c \\\\ 6t+4 &=-c\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=2{t}^{2}.$ Substituting $c=2{t}^{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6t+4 & =-2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+6t+4 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}+3t+2 & =0 \\\\ (t+2)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the points corresponding to $t=-2$ and $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3}(-1)^{3},3(-1)^{2}+4(-1)\\right\\rangle =\\left\\langle -\\frac{2}{3},-1\\right\\rangle , \\\\ r(-2) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3}(-2)^{3},3(-2)^{2}+4(-2)\\right\\rangle =\\left\\langle -\\frac{16}{3},4\\right\\rangle ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $\\left(-\\frac{2}{3},-1\\right)$ and $\\left(-\\frac{16}{3},4\\right).$"] [:db/cas 17592186503750 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{5}+5x+3}{{x}^{4}-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{x+0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{5}+5x+3}{{x}^{4}-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{\\left(x+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{x+0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503848 :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=6$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{6+1}}{6+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 7{x}^{6} \\\\ &= {x}^{6}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the power rule with $n=6$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{6+1}}{6+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}+C\\right) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 7{x}^{6} \\\\ &= {x}^{6}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503851 :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=7$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{7+1}}{7+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{8}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 8{x}^{7} \\\\ &= {x}^{7}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the power rule with $n=7$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{7+1}}{7+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}+C\\right) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{8}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{8}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 8{x}^{7} \\\\ &= {x}^{7}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503854 :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=5$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{5+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6{x}^{5} \\\\ &= {x}^{5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the power rule with $n=5$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{5+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C\\right) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6{x}^{5} \\\\ &= {x}^{5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503860 :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=8$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{8+1}}{8+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{9}}{9}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{9}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{9}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 9{x}^{8} \\\\ &= {x}^{8}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the power rule with $n=8$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{8+1}}{8+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{9}}{9}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{9}}{9}+C\\right) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{9}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 9{x}^{8} \\\\ &= {x}^{8}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503871 :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=3$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\ &= {x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We apply the power rule with $n=3$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C\\right) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\ &= {x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503879 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}={x}^{5/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2+1}}{(\\frac{5}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}{x}^{7/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{5/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}={x}^{5/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2+1}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C\\right) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}{x}^{7/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{5/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503882 :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C\\right) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503887 :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C\\right) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503893 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{9}}={x}^{9/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{9}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{9}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{9/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/2+1}}{(\\frac{9}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{11/2}}{\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{11}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{11/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{11}\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}{x}^{11/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{9/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{9}}={x}^{9/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{9}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{9}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{9/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/2+1}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}+1\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{11/2}}{\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}+C\\right) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{11}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{11/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{11}\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}{x}^{11/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{9/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186503894 :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}={x}^{7/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{7}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{7/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5+1}}{(\\frac{7}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{12/5}}{\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating